window.harnessChunks["rows-0d7678185e8d3b57.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:50:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/beipo-ai/NorthCUA\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NorthCUA</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/beipo-ai/NorthCUA/tree/fb5f9e2725c7cfe268fa0c2dc40f44cc132c12f4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fb5f9e2725c7cfe268fa0c2dc40f44cc132c12f4\">fb5f9e2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out one user computer-use task through observed Desktop/Web state and model-chosen actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown application screens, changed DOM/state, unexpected tool responses, context growth, unsatisfied task conditions and execution interruptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next computer-use/tool action or finish based on observed state and returned tool results. - Decision owner: Configured agent model operating in the first-party Desktop/Web round executor; external inference service supplies model computation but does not implement the NorthCUA task loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: NexAU-compatible model adapter, typed tool contracts, context/image window, working notes, action backend, controller/device bridge, step budgets and persisted history. - Closure path: User request → contextual observation → model step/action choice → constrained tool execution → new screenshot/DOM/tool result and notes → subsequent model step or terminal result. - Boundary reachability: The documented local Web/Desktop CLI and remote task modes instantiate this actor loop using bundled AgentKit specs and first-party tool dispatch; no test-only harness or adjacent research agent is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects substantive environment actions after seeing observations. Tool schemas, transport, budget and safety gates constrain or carry this discretion rather than select it. - Evidence: Web agent specification, Desktop step executor, runtime architecture. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the first-party model/tool loop; no live hosted-provider success claim. - Caveats: S1 depends on configured model/device/backend availability, and the assessment does not measure task efficacy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate interference between distinct simultaneously admitted batch task attempts, especially duplicate execution, resource oversubscription and accidental state reuse. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent attempts may claim the same task, worker/device capacity or prepared environment after restarts, timeouts and parallel preparation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select or revise which attempt can claim a lease and when backpressured capacity may admit subsequent tasks; a higher-level autonomous coordination policy would have to be composed to own the discretion. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic scheduler, leases and persisted rules select immediate admission based on human/developer-provided limits; there is no demonstrated autonomous agent choosing inter-task coordination policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite task claim/heartbeat, attempt identity, task-scoped sandbox/device lease, backpressure samples, single-use prepared reservation and reconciliation. - Closure path: Contending batch attempts → exclusive task/lease claim and controller-pressure check → one attempt proceeds, conflicting contenders wait or reconcile → subsequent tasks claim only released/eligible capacity. - Boundary reachability: Batch mode ships these scheduler/lease components and documented resume semantics; unlike a hypothetical generic queue, the code specifically handles simultaneous task claims and attempt reuse. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Concrete inter-S1 conflict attenuation exists, but its decisive runtime rule is deterministic; a first-party S2-specific constructor path is exposed without a shipped model-owned policy adjustment loop. - Distinct S1 units: Independently running Desktop/Web task attempts with their own task goals, environments and actor loops under a single batch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Duplicate task submissions or competing sandbox/device admissions and reused application state when attempts overlap or a runner resumes. - Attenuating coordination relation: Unique task/attempt claims and task-scoped leases, bounded concurrency, backpressure gating and selected retry/reconcile rules. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Denied/late claims do not start duplicate actor operations; capacity availability and persisted task status determine later submission and resume. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The coordination enforces a named cross-attempt collision and resource contention invariant and feeds a concrete admission/withholding result into subsequent distinct task attempts. - Evidence: batch scheduler, lease coordinator, backpressure gate, batch recovery documentation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for an actual inter-attempt coordination path, medium for constructor classification. - Caveats: The batch is optional; an individual local run has no distinct S2 peer set. Deterministic conflict avoidance cannot be upgraded to S2=A without autonomous coordination discretion.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Whole-batch current commitments and running capacity can be supervised by a legitimate operator who controls admission, pause, cancellation, drain and selected retries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failed or uncertain attempts, exhausted retry budgets, workload pressure, controller interruptions and cleanup problems across a batch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: View current aggregate task statuses and choose to stop new admissions, cancel current work, drain running tasks, resume or authorize specific failed-task retries. - Decision owner: The first-party mode&#39;s human batch operator. The scheduler and SQLite state apply that returned decision; no independent AI whole-batch manager is shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Status/report CLI, persisted control flags and task states, pause/drain/cancel handlers, selected-retry plan validation and scheduler poller. - Closure path: Operator reviews whole-batch status/report → invokes pause/drain/cancel or bounded selected retry/resume → persistent flags/attempt ceilings are read by the live scheduler → current/new task admissions and execution change. - Boundary reachability: The documented northcua batch status|report|pause|drain|cancel|resume commands and scheduler&#39;s control-flag poller form a usable first-party operator mode over the same persisted batch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is an operationally returned parent mode (P), not automatic agent S3; deterministic capacity thresholds and external operator actions do not supply autonomous S3=A or C by themselves. - Whole-system current view: Persisted all-task states and report/failure breakdown across one identified batch, including attempts and evaluation statuses. - Current-control decision scope: Stop or resume new admissions, cancel active work, drain committed tasks and select permitted retries for failed tasks, affecting whole-batch present commitments. - Evidence: batch operator commands, CLI branch handling, control-flag scheduler, selected retry plan. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; owner/control return is directly coded, but no actual operator run was reproduced in this assessment. - Caveats: Parent governance is only at a single batch&#39;s current-operations recursion, not at the OSS project&#39;s policy/identity recursion. This finding does not classify arbitrary status dashboards as S3.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: A separate evaluator agent challenges actor completion against independently gathered artifact and optionally live application reality. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent self-report may disagree with task requirements, unsaved application state, missing artifacts or unseen outcome failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select relevant raw/recorded evidence and optional live probes, judge requirements and submit a structured success/failure report distinct from actor termination. - Decision owner: The separately instantiated first-party model-driven evaluator, through its own tool-selection and final-report loop; not the actor, trace writer or deterministic batch status projector. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Evidence manifest, read-only artifact and live probe tools, bounded evaluator step execution, report schema, fail-open/fail-closed configuration and batch result projection. - Closure path: Actor completion → independent artifact/live evidence collection → evaluator model verdict in finalreport → recorded outcome distinct from actor claim → batch task becomes success/failure/pending and controls downstream retry/reporting decisions. - Boundary reachability: Shipped local/remote Web/Desktop evaluator modes can be enabled in app configuration or CLI, and the Web evaluator runs before releasing the actor&#39;s original browser session. A direct live probe is conditional on explicit permission; the artifact-evidence route remains independently wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The evaluator chooses direct evidence inspection and a task-grounded judgment in its own model/tool loop; its report changes terminal status. Deterministic tooling does not make that independent audit judgment. - Claim being audited: Whether the actor actually satisfied the user&#39;s task despite its own reported completion. - Ordinary reporting path: Actor terminal result, its model/tool traces, screenshots and action outputs. - Complementary access path: Separate evaluator&#39;s evidence selection and tool access to saved artifacts plus, when enabled, fresh DOM/screenshot or allowed device read-only probes against the original live session. - Independence boundary: The evaluator executes in a separate model-round/runtime and has evidence/probe tools unavailable to ordinary self-report; it is still a first-party evaluation path, not an externally independent scientific audit or proof of blind reviewer independence. - Who acts on findings: Batch status projection treats a present negative verdict as failure, while operator/retry flow may admit a subsequent attempt; no claim that the same actor is automatically corrected before finalization. - Evidence: evaluator loop, Web evaluator service, read-only probe contracts, terminal projection, evaluation modes. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for independently wired model audit and returned verdict, not for real-world judge accuracy. - Caveats: Evaluation may be disabled or fail open; live probes require permission and original session availability; a status verdict affects subsequent batch handling rather than guaranteeing a corrective rerun. No actual hosted model, external evaluator or empirical accuracy study was executed for this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped S4-specific prospective external-environment model and adaptation-option decision returning to present organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in apps/sites, task complexity and model quality are seen by current actor/evaluator, but not separately explored as future environment adaptation at the batch/harness recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Generate future-facing capability/policy adaptation options from external distinctions and negotiate them with current operational control. - Decision owner: Project developers/operators may separately plan upgrades; actor plans current tasks, evaluator judges completed work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Working notes, image/context compaction, feedback artifacts, model/provider configuration, benchmark and research documentation. - Closure path: Task observations and evaluator reports feed current run/batch status, not a first-party outside-and-then option/implementation return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task planning, context memory, runtime retries and post-run quality reporting are not themselves prospective adaptation judgments. - Evidence: system tour, context mechanisms, batch lifecycle. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the reviewed distribution. - Caveats: Research and model upgrades can support future work outside this runtime boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No system-level identity or ultimate-policy resolution channel with a legitimate deciding owner and returned binding decision is shipped for the assessed task/batch organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risky computer actions, provider/device access, evaluation-probe mutability and cost/time limits are bounded by operator-set configuration and tool controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Adjudicate contested identity/purpose/ultimate-policy matters and return binding changed policy to the running organization. - Decision owner: Users/developers choose static configuration and external provider access; no first-party S5 decision actor or parent appeal path is established for such matters. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only evaluator probes by default, explicit mutating-tool opt-in, sandbox capabilities, model/task budgets, run authorization and cancellation. - Closure path: Current operator configuration → enforcement of permitted tools/budgets, with no identity-level proposal → parent authority → decision → changed operation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ordinary permission and task cancellation choices are not ultimate-policy decisions, and no shipped independent policy/identity deliberation return is identified. - Evidence: evaluation configuration, batch configuration, architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high at assessed operation/batch boundary. - Caveats: Project maintainers naturally retain licensing/release governance, but that adjacent project-level authority cannot be silently credited as runtime S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0ad99b51aef44dd4a35185e7ecf73717a6d29557/assessments/no/northcua.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0ad99b51aef44dd4a35185e7ecf73717a6d29557\">0ad99b5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:13:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/halayhq/halay\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">halay</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/halayhq/halay/tree/49bccfcb75e805b7ab55bb40c92a109dcdc62c24\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 49bccfcb75e805b7ab55bb40c92a109dcdc62c24\">49bccfc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Distinct user-task agents operate in their assigned project directories through the launched configured CLI. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing user requests, project state and subprocess/tool feedback delivered to each CLI session. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Each running model-backed CLI agent chooses task actions and next turns, while halay controls the first-party spawning, routing, replies and child communication interface. - Decision owner: Individual CLI-backed model task actor, through halay&#39;s installed multi-agent composition; not Node HTTP request routing alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Backends, per-agent cwd/account/model, MCP swarm tools, event stream, permissions and optional worktrees. - Closure path: User/conductor task→halay launches addressed CLI agent→upstream engine acts in workspace→result/status returns via halay→model continues, receives follow-up or returns result. - Boundary reachability: src/server.js launches supported CLI processes and src/swarm-mcp.js exposes actionable tools; this requires one external CLI installed and authenticated, and does not credit its underlying internal tool loop as a first-party implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Real CLI-backed models have discretion over work; the first-party control plane enables and observes their running roles. The launcher alone would not be autonomous. - Evidence: spawn and task lifecycle, backends, swarm tools. - Basis: Supported executable agent-CLI composition and returned operational state, not claimed first-party model internals. - Confidence: Medium-high for first-party runtime integration, not upstream vendor correctness. - Caveats: The prerequisite external CLI supplies cognition, local tool semantics and vendor behavior; absent a configured backend, halay is an idle control plane.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately demonstrated autonomous attenuation of an observed conflict among the multiple operating child units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Shared-workspace collisions and competing task dependencies are plausible; the default task DAG orders work but is authored and assigned as a workflow, not measured inter-S1 contention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party dependency gate rejects blocked starts; conductor can manually/model-select routing as S3 rather than independent S2 balancing. - Decision owner: User/conductor for assignments, deterministic task dependency guard for eligibility. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task graph dependson, worktrees, agent message bus, parent-child scoped tools and queues. - Closure path: Task dependency→ready/waiting/blocked gate or manual assignment; messages travel between actors, but no installed contention sensing→S2 arbitration→revised peer behavior loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool messages, worktree isolation and static dependency checks are valuable coordination infrastructure without evidence of a specific separately owned S2 regulator. - Evidence: task dependency/agent management, swarm tool interface. - Basis: Bounded absence after testing strongest task-DAG and peer-messaging candidates. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A conductor can respond to a user-observed conflict, but no distinct routine S2 feedback mandate was found in the shipped setup.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Shipped model-driven conductor supervises the present agent fleet and allocates/revises task commitments and capacity. - Whole-system current view: Conductor&#39;s listagents, task listing, status/attention, child-result notifications and account availability expose the bounded installed fleet and current work backlog, rather than a single worker&#39;s perspective. - Current-control decision scope: Conductor can choose and revise work-to-child allocation, active agent count/model/folder, follow-up task instructions and interruption in response to observed fleet state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent user requests across projects, idle/busy/blocked children, failed tasks, approval pauses, delayed results and current model/account capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Conductor chooses whether to create or reuse an agent, what folder/model/account to assign, when to message or interrupt it and how to react to its returned result/status. - Decision owner: Conductor model running with the bundled role prompt and privileged first-party swarm MCP rights; runtime enforces access scopes and completion notifications. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listagents, startagent, sendmessage, readagent, task assignment, stop/interrupt, child-finish notifications, persistent fleet state and task dependency gates. - Closure path: User&#39;s multi-project goal→conductor lists fleet/task state→selects agent and assignment→agent acts→halay notifies its completion/error/block→conductor revises instructions, assignments or informs user. - Boundary reachability: A shipped Swarm chat has its own conductor role and tool permissions; unlike a passive dashboard it can model-select concrete task allocation from returned runtime state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party conductor prompt gives the model an operational portfolio mandate and action rights; subprocess state and database enforcement support rather than replace its choices. - Evidence: conductor mandate, scoped MCP actions, fleet/task state and wakeup, notification format. - Basis: Reachable first-party conductor perception→discretionary allocation/revision→child-action→status feedback over a bounded fleet. - Confidence: High for installed mandate and capability, not measured real fleet performance. - Caveats: The conductor&#39;s decision model runs via a required external CLI and may fail to act on feedback; human controls, context budgets and per-child permissions constrain it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent alternate evidence path produces an audit verdict returned to conductor beyond ordinary agent and tool reports. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False child completion claims and unsafe changes could be reviewed by humans; no separate installed auditing reviewer independently samples operational reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Conductor can read child transcript, summaries and git status, but this is ordinary supervision; approvals are chosen by the human. - Decision owner: Conductor for monitoring and operator for approvals; no complementary auditor with independent judgment/return authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent transcript, worktree state, browser UI, status notifications, reviewable git changes and authentication. - Closure path: Child events→conductor/UI; no independent artifact sampling/replay→adversarial audit finding→corrective S3 loop in standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate transcript reader does not gain a distinct independent observational channel, and the human may optionally review but is outside first-party installed audit. - Evidence: swarm read/status, event handling. - Basis: Bounded absence review of strongest transcript/worktree evidence paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External CLI vendors or human code review may supply external auditors, not credited to halay by default.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No continuous prospective environment analysis selects future capability/organizational changes for this fleet. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New CLI backends, future user work, schedule timing, model prices and capacity changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator configures accounts/backends and schedules; conductor handles current task choices and user-described future jobs. - Decision owner: Human operator for product/organizational evolution, conductor model for present tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Backend discovery, scheduled/recurring tasks, durable bots, peer configuration and model selection. - Closure path: User configuration/schedule→current or timed execution; no outside environmental forecast→strategic option selection→negotiation with current S3 and adopted future operating rule. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Scheduled execution and selectable backends are operational capacity, not autonomous anticipation of changing external circumstances. - Evidence: server and task schedule, conductor instructions, bots. - Basis: Bounded absence review including strongest persistent/scheduled mode. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Conductor can be asked to research future changes as a one-off S1 work item; no permanent S4 function follows.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No distinct legitimate ultimate identity-policy role owns and closes an organizational charter/mandate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Privileged agent management, resource/cost limits, malicious agents, user identity/authentication and permission escalation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authenticated human owner sets controls; static role access enforces conductor versus child scope, and the user decides tool approvals. - Decision owner: Operator for legitimate ultimate constraints, deterministic auth/MCP guard for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Passkeys, per-agent bearer tokens, parent ownership checks, approval mode, model/account boundaries and persistent configuration. - Closure path: Operator setting or approval→guarded API behavior; no autonomous or parent-assisted identity-policy deliberation and returned authoritative mandate to S3/S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Strong security and an exclusive conductor role are not sufficient for an independent governance legitimacy cycle. - Evidence: auth/access, MCP scoping, server. - Basis: Bounded absence after screening legitimate operator policy and role privilege paths. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A human-led institution could govern an installed halay fleet; it is not a supplied self-governing S5 actor.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0d074c28204362cf5ef61e4df5c16812954ee014/assessments/ha/halay.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0d074c28204362cf5ef61e4df5c16812954ee014\">0d074c2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T17:08:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lose4578/Auriga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Auriga</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lose4578/Auriga/tree/3e1be75e186e49f8c268fd5fc6c08427f99254ce\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3e1be75e186e49f8c268fd5fc6c08427f99254ce\">3e1be75 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: pursue robot-manipulation goals through visual observation, task planning, multi-stage preview/execution, measured action feedback and recovery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing object/robot geometry, failed or partial grasps, occlusion, movement slips, constrained arm trajectories and uncertain action completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next physical task action, observation, program, trajectory revision or recovery in light of returned evidence. - Decision owner: primary model-driven Codex actor (named Astra in optional verification documentation), with action authority mediated by the Harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: action identity and current-observation checks, native motion planner, gripper guards, single action writer, program review broker, action ledger, wall/budget supervision and the physical simulator. - Closure path: Codex task/plan → robotobserve and robotaction preview → model selects execute → Harness.execute native worker → measured receipt/images/stop reason → model adjusts or terminates episode. - Boundary reachability: normal gateway creates the Codex session and Harness, registers robotaction and connects execution to native RoboDojo worker. The model/tool loop is shipped with documented flags, not merely a benchmark test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static robot safety gates do not select a new manipulation strategy; the actor model makes the task-level decisions even when a hard guard prevents unsafe execution. - Evidence: README execution loop, Harness, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct conflict-specific inter-S1 coordination and return is established in the supported robot actor organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dual-arm trajectory interference and concurrent program/native action requests are real actuator/resource hazards but not conflicts among distinct autonomous S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: arbitrate independent agent commitments and return a coordination resolution; not evidenced. - Decision owner: one actor plans both arms; deterministic Harness serializes physical actions and rejects stale plans, rather than an S2 coordination owner regulating competing agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stage-order dependencies, dual-arm path preview, active-writer exclusivity, plan revision, program broker approval and stop gates. - Closure path: native constraints and the primary actor can alter a manipulation task, but no separate inter-S1 interference-resolution result is fed into distinct operational agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: two robot arms are not independent goal-directed S1 cells; an optional evidence agent audits a claim but does not compete for physical task execution. - Evidence: unified actions, Harness action writer, program review. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no independent whole-organization current-control authority over a population of operational S1 commitments is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: native action budget, simulator step accounting, active action conflicts, unknown stop confirmation, model transport failures and episode wall limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select systemic allocation/priority/commitment intervention across distinct operations; absent beyond the single robot episode&#39;s work plan. - Decision owner: primary actor owns task strategy; Harness enforces state/timeout/cost constraints; operator defines task/benchmark configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: supervision.waitturn, action gate, per-action budget, program source grants, GPU resource checks and status/feedback records. - Closure path: failures can stop one episode or prompt its actor to try another action, not a separate whole-current S3 decision that regulates multiple autonomous units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: counters, stop gates and request retry policy are enforcement; removing a hypothetical S3 model leaves them intact. - Evidence: runtime supervision, Harness, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: the primary robot actor&#39;s local claim that a selected grasp/lift/transfer/place/release or retreat phase achieved its expected visible result, as distinct from a native worker&#39;s narrower completed execution receipt. - Ordinary reporting path: Harness.execute returns a native action receipt and current measured observation to the primary actor, which can then report success on the strength of its own readings; a tool response alone does not prove object retention or task completion. - Complementary access path: the separately launched Sol reviewer is given the raw linked BEFORE/AFTER permitted RGB images, measured robot state, immutable action IDs/receipts and the actor&#39;s intended outcome as a hypothesis; it checks visual identity/relation independently and returns a structured met/notmet/uncertain verdict with gaps. - Independence boundary: CodexEvidenceAgent creates a fresh ephemeral thread, empty workspace and isolated home, disables memories/tools/hooks, and rejects observed helper tool activity; it is not forked from the acting Codex history and cannot issue motion commands. The actor chooses checkpoint and evidence, limiting scope but not transferring visual judgment to the actor. - Who acts on findings: the primary Astra actor receives visionreview results in its ordinary tool stream and is instructed to resolve unmet/uncertain evidence with fresh observations or changed recovery; the reviewer itself cannot execute or compel a trajectory and the operating loop retains physical action ownership. - Function: provide an independent, evidence-limited second opinion on a manipulation operation&#39;s claimed visible outcome and send discrepancies or uncertainty back to the acting agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic success interpretations of completed motion receipts, wrong target identity, occlusion, unsupported grasp/placement/release claims and partial execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge met, notmet or uncertain for a claimed phase by comparing independent before/after RGB, execution receipts and measured robot state, and specify evidence gaps/recovery feedback. - Decision owner: separate fresh gpt-6.1-sol evidence agent; primary Astra remains owner of the subsequent physical action and the operator decides whether to enable this configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic provenance and schema checks, isolated fresh home/thread, suppressed tools/memory, payload source hashes, shared model budget, exact model assertion and unsupported-positive veto in constrainanswer. - Closure path: actor selects meaningful checkpoint and invokes visionreview(evidencemode=&#39;operation&#39;) → fresh Sol independently examines linked images/receipts and issues bounded verdict and follow-up → tool response returns to the primary actor&#39;s Codex turn → actor instructed to resolve uncertainty or choose a changed recovery before later motions. The auditor never executes robot actions. - Boundary reachability: features.visualreviewmodel=&#39;gpt-6.1-sol&#39; is a documented supported config; the actual Workbench instantiates VisualReviewer(CodexEvidenceAgent(session)), the runtime tool handler dispatches it and returns the structured assessment to the ordinary actor context. Default null mode has no S3 helper; positive vector reflects the first-party enabled operating mode, not default execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the fresh model provides the discretionary visual evidence judgment; deterministic validation limits unsupported assertions but cannot itself interpret ambiguous target retention/phase outcome. Actor authority to choose recovery is distinct from auditor judgment ownership. - Evidence: mode and return contract, Workbench construction, fresh agent isolation, review judgment, actor recovery instructions, tool handler. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: actor chooses evidence/request and need not obey a fixed compulsory verification phase sequence; this is a first-party supported optional independent second-opinion loop, not privileged simulator-ground-truth audit or guaranteed physical correction. No real provider/robot verification was executed here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate externally prospective adaptation process with authorized whole-runtime capability-choice and return is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel object geometries, repeated failed grasps, changing camera viewpoints, different robot scenes and future task transfer. - Decisive decision or feedback right: change organizational operating capability based on prospective environmental alternatives; not evidenced at the assessed one-episode recursion. - Decision owner: primary model adapts task actions in the current scene; developers/operators configure perception services, model and robot plugins. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: adaptiveactioncheckpoint failure reminders, observation memory, trajectory cost previews, program synthesis and optional external perception. - Closure path: measured failures can prompt an immediate alternate grasp, viewpoint or trajectory within one task; no distinct environment/future analysis → approved organization-wide capability adaptation → feedback loop is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adaptive recovery within one operating episode is S1 learning/action selection, not automatically S4 strategic adaptation. - Evidence: checkpoint helper, model-led recovery, unified actions/memory. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate organizational policy/identity arbitration with legitimate authoritative return is implemented inside the robot episode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: robot safety boundaries, model permissions, execution modes, privileged task truth, tool grants and physical stop decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adjudicate identity-level commitments and revise binding policy for the whole; not shown beyond fixed configuration and task controls. - Decision owner: developer/operator specifies guardrails and modes; deterministic Harness enforces validation; primary actor decides ordinary manipulation steps within those bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: actor observation redaction, action schema and state locks, program grants, model call budget and sandbox controls. - Closure path: individual actions are admitted/rejected against predefined rules, but no policy-level dispute → qualified authority → ultimate decision → new identity-governing operating rule is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may choose a risky trajectory and receive advisory data, but it does not own the system&#39;s ultimate identity or change invariant control policy. - Evidence: action gate, actor information boundary, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ec6167d00afe015e5c030fd19139e421de731e13/assessments/au/auriga.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ec6167d00afe015e5c030fd19139e421de731e13\">ec6167d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T17:57:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mihazs/agent-harness-lab\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Harness Lab</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mihazs/agent-harness-lab/tree/1b165594818bab9f92605fb4bd5ec0c6f943c2fc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1b165594818bab9f92605fb4bd5ec0c6f943c2fc\">1b16559 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: investigate bounded harness defects and generate an executable candidate instruction/skill change against project evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown agent failure mechanisms, contradictory observations, ineffective instructions, regressions and source-context gaps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the proposer model chooses which supported mechanism to address and what candidate file edits to stage; baseline/candidate model task executors choose actions under identical frozen tasks and runtime settings. - Decision owner: selected Codex/Claude/Responses model-backed proposer and task roles in explicitly invoked improve mode; first-party controller scopes and executes them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project-local snapshots and path allowlists, model role prompts, source/analysis receipts, artifact hashes, shared trial/time ceiling, frozen candidate, paired executors and evaluation feedback. - Closure path: collect bounded evidence → proposer model selects candidate → change is frozen → baseline and candidate agent trials execute → observed failures/regressions feed the bounded refinement or final no-change/recommendation. - Boundary reachability: workflow.py invokes native/Responses role runner in preparation and task phases; ahl improve is the documented CLI entry, provided runtime availability and permissions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: proposer decides semantic repair hypotheses and actual candidate contents, not just a deterministic suggested text; analyze, static classifiers and test bookkeeping alone would be constructors, not S1 A. - Evidence: proposer dispatch and trial flow, controlled role preparation, documented modes. - Basis: pinned first-party execution code and runtime contract; not a live paid Codex/Claude experiment here. - Confidence: high that model-backed roles are shipped, medium for real-world repair quality. - Caveats: no model calls in analyze or offline diagnostics; no general automatic promotion implied by successful paired tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no separately established inter-S1 reciprocal conflict-attenuation loop beyond task role isolation and experiment scheduling at the defined single-run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a proposer could contaminate evaluator inputs or a concurrent improvement run could alter the same project, but AHL prevents those situations by frozen views/locks rather than demonstrated feedback among independently operating S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: controller grants separate role paths, budgets, frozen snapshots and project locks; a stale or leaked input blocks the whole evaluation. - Decision owner: deterministic custody/permission controller, without cross-worker conflict-specific negotiation or corrective return to both S1 cells. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role-scoped views, frozen task banks and hashes, project lock, candidate/baseline isolation, source-drift checks, sequential trial orchestration. - Closure path: potential cross-role contamination is prevented or detected; invalid runs stop or require intervention. This is evaluation integrity, not evidenced coordination of independent operational commitments. - Boundary reachability: role views, frozen candidate and validation are shipped; a separate correction negotiation between proposer and evaluator is not. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct fresh roles, sequential stages and file isolation are important safeguards, but not enough to infer VSM S2 without a concrete disturbance-feedback relation returning to affected S1 units. - Evidence: role construction and separation, controlled execution boundaries, preflight/custody policy. - Basis: bounded negative review of multi-role experiment/custody paths. - Confidence: medium-high for this recursion. - Caveats: a future multi-project adaptation fleet could implement true S2; that is outside this single-project run.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate the active run&#39;s preparation/evaluation roles, resource budget, candidate eligibility, current evidence and terminal disposition, with user-owned runtime/scope initiation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: budget exhaustion, changed source files, invalid evaluator controls, failing task bank, unavailable authentication, partial runs and repeated monitoring without new evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: controller freezes/validates candidate and grader inputs, schedules tests/refinement, rejects a candidate or emits recommendations; human owner authorizes project, chosen runtime, scope and any later behavioral adoption. - Decision owner: deterministic workflow.py and promotion prerequisites (C), with human operator (P) initiating and deciding outside-CLI adoption. Controller does not quietly grant itself publication rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project locks, shared 45-minute/24-role defaults, checksums, state receipts, role budgets, graded trial coverage, early fail-closed exits, optional monitor checkpoints. - Closure path: current evidence and role result → controller authorizes next preparation/test/stop or reports unsupported → stored checkpoint/receipt constrains the next agent dispatch. - Boundary reachability: ahl improve and ahl watch use these live controller functions; watch is foreground and must be explicitly started. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-run scheduling/disposition is rule-based despite model roles doing task work; real human controls authenticated invocation and reviewed candidate adoption. - Whole-system current view: baseline/candidate hashes, source identity, proposer/evaluator states, task bank coverage, model calls, time/trial remaining, results and previous monitor checkpoints. - Current-control decision scope: allow/deny a role run, protect frozen inputs, retry once, stop on invalid evidence, write final review/recommendation and watch restart suppression. - Evidence: controller loop, invocation preflight, monitoring mode. - Basis: first-party deterministic role/state controller. - Confidence: high in structural control rights; actual credentialed runs not repeated here. - Caveats: human adoption beyond CLI and real trusted production integration are not claimed as observed automatic completion.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: a separately prompted evaluator-author agent constructs tests/graders from baseline/source evidence while deliberately blind to the candidate, and the controller executes those frozen evaluators to challenge the proposer&#39;s claimed improvement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: proposer self-praise, overfit instruction rewrites, candidate-specific tests, evaluator leakage and differences that disappear on held-out/regression cases. - Decisive decision or feedback right: evaluator-author model independently chooses test cases and grader construction within the baseline evidence and broken/partial/complete control contract; frozen graders then measure actual paired task outputs and can cause rejection or bounded refinement. - Decision owner: separate fresh model-backed evaluator-author role owns substantive test design; deterministic comparison controller enforces controls and verdict/disposition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate role workspace/views, baseline-only source context, withheld candidate and held-out inputs, grader self-tests, manifest freezing, task result artifacts, same-runtime paired trials and custody checks. - Closure path: proposer freezes candidate/claim → independently scoped author designs tests without seeing patch → controls/graders are frozen → baseline/candidate execute → actual result comparison rejects/permits refinement or emits review recommendation. - Boundary reachability: workflow.py constructs a separate evaluator session in native mode or construction.author/controlled role access, and validates a manifest before paired trials. No optional uninstalled reviewer service is required beyond the chosen agent runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deciding what independent probes should measure involves a different model context, not simply running a copy of the proposer&#39;s test; the final grader/veto itself is deterministic, so this is not automatically a certified independent human scientific audit. - Claim being audited: a proposed harness instruction/skill edit improves required task outcomes without regression under comparable inputs. - Ordinary reporting path: proposer mechanisms, candidate diff, self-declared proposal.json and any development trial feedback. - Complementary access path: evaluator author receives baseline/source/analysis without candidate patch, builds unrelated executable tasks and graders, and the controller examines fresh baseline/candidate task artifacts. - Independence boundary: roles are separately scoped and patch-blind by construction, but cooperative Codex/Claude runtime/hook behavior and model provider isolation are not proven adversarial security; some source metadata is shared. - Who acts on findings: controller rejects/refines/recommends; human decides any behavioral adoption. No claim of certified unconditional promotion. - Evidence: independent evaluation-construction prompt and calls, role-specific controlled author, frozen task method. - Basis: explicit shipped two-role model construction plus executable artifact feedback, not observed scientific effect size. - Confidence: medium-high for independent input view and actual verdict path, medium for model quality and native role isolation. - Caveats: “fresh session” alone is not a proof of full root independence or test correctness; graders need explicit self-tests and absence of leakage.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no completed behavioral adoption return turning evaluated future-harness candidates into active project operating capability under the shipped CLI&#39;s general workflow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolving task types and systematic future agent behavior might warrant new harness instructions and skills. - Decisive decision or feedback right: proposer can forecast candidate effects and evaluation compares development/regression/held-out tasks, but improve deliberately returns a review recommendation instead of applying behavioral changes; the human may later edit active files separately. - Decision owner: user/project outside the CLI owns actual behavioral promotion; no first-party general S4 decision-to-adoption loop has authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: retrospective analysis, selected candidate proposals, prospective task banks, held-out controls, promotion prerequisites and fail-closed recommendation status. - Closure path: candidate → prospective paired task evidence → review recommendation; missing final behavioral activation in the same supported controller means the full outside-and-then capability return is unclosed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: creating and evaluating future options is substantial S4-adjacent intelligence but cannot be scored as completed S4 when the proposed capability is explicitly not adopted. Narrow structural BOM repair is not general behavioral S4. - Evidence: README no auto-adoption, promotion policy recommendation-only, one-call narrow repair. - Basis: explicit runtime/README negative authority and supported-mode examination. - Confidence: high for CLI&#39;s deliberate blocked behavioral return; a manual fallback may differ under explicit user authority. - Caveats: installed harness-self-improvement skill describes assisted evaluation/editing; without a validated matching runtime/receipt this is not imported as general autonomous promotion into the guarded ahl improve path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate whole-project identity/ultimate-purpose adjudication loop evidenced in one harness optimization run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: privacy of traces, scope of external models, credentialed cost, whether an evaluator can be trusted and changes to global instructions are real governance concerns. - Decisive decision or feedback right: invocation permissions and configured scope restrict project files and runtime capabilities, while the user decides model/provider, accepts adoption and can stop the command; none is charter-level identity resolution returned as binding system purpose. - Decision owner: outside user/project authority; controller enforces safety/eligibility preconditions rather than deliberating ultimate purpose. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project path allowlists, no global skills by default, no publication rights, credential-bound external calls, privacy-safe artifacts and explicit fail-closed prerequisites. - Closure path: invalid permission/role scope blocks the run or preserves unmodified files; no first-party identity dispute reaches a higher legitimate policy owner and returns as an ultimate revised charter. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: safety and provenance constraints limit experiments but are not themselves S5 governance. - Evidence: scope and limits, preflight boundaries, promotion authority. - Basis: bounded negative review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the human project owner has external policy rights; those are not automatically first-party runtime S5(P).\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/19d30e2238e1d50e87e240bcf67ab7b02ca7f8f6/assessments/ag/agent-harness-lab.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 19d30e2238e1d50e87e240bcf67ab7b02ca7f8f6\">19d30e2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:08:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJUZhiyuCai/bread-companion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bread Companion</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJUZhiyuCai/bread-companion/tree/058ad153e27bd6470a7e5ef4cab778a93d659a6d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 058ad153e27bd6470a7e5ef4cab778a93d659a6d\">058ad15 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous bounded conversation and evidence-task transformation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different user material, missing/conflicting evidence, rejected interpretations and malformed or inappropriate generated candidates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose supported findings, wording, draft structure or a practice question; revise after concrete rejection/validation feedback. - Decision owner: the configured real-agent model within the first-party generation/repair loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mode prompts, selected source segments, schema/evidence/style validators, two-attempt repair, run IDs and client lifecycle checks. - Closure path: validation errors re-enter a new generator attempt; accepted content becomes current findings/card/practice state, influencing later turns. - Boundary reachability: real-agent.generate implements the provider interface used by the deployed server; BreadController submits a current turn and reducer applies only matching epoch/context results. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model determines substantive task content and correction, while the controller enforces validity and human save authority. Fixed UI transitions alone could not produce the same discretionary outcomes. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; prompts/agent.ts; domain/evidence.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: no general tool-executing autonomy; drafts require user confirmation for persistence. This is not a therapy efficacy or clinical safety claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct operational-unit coordination path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These are one conversational work cell&#39;s pipeline/transport stages and revision-consistency controls. No distinct purposeful operational S1 cells with an inter-S1 disturbance/attenuation relation were found. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; services/run-controller.ts; services/call-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-current organizational control function established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These regulate individual requests, versions, service capacity and task consent. A session view or stop button is not by itself whole-system resource/commitment regulation with a function-specific parent decision loop. - Evidence: services/bread-controller.ts; services/run-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts; server/limits.ts; server/admission.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: narrow complementary semantic audit of whether contested analysis reintroduces the user&#39;s denied interpretation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a candidate can pass schema/verbatim/lexical checks yet paraphrase or recommend the rejected interpretation again. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently compare actual candidate with user rejection record and judge semantic consistency. - Decision owner: the model in the fresh rejection-review invocation; runtime parses/enforces its verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate review prompt/request, only candidate/rejection/current user data, bounded audit input, conservative verdict parsing and forced retry/fallback. - Closure path: inconsistent/invalid audit adds REJECTEDINTERPRETATIONREPEATED; generator receives explicit corrective feedback; after a second repeated rejection the product returns quote/unknown fallback rather than display the contested interpretation. - Boundary reachability: RealAgentProvider invokes the separate reviewer only for non-listen analysis with rejectedFindings after normal validation; reducer REJECT and context-builder persist/send that rejection record. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the independent review call supplies semantic judgment beyond the generator&#39;s candidate and deterministic lexical check; parsing is enforcement, not the owner. No independent provider identity is claimed. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; server/providers/rejection-review.ts; domain/reducer.ts; domain/context-builder.ts; domain/evidence.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: same configured model/provider may serve both roles; independence is fresh context and task separation for this narrow consistency claim, not empirical independence from shared model biases. Ordinary chat is unaudited. - Claim being audited: the candidate respects the user&#39;s previously rejected interpretation and its paraphrases. - Ordinary reporting path: generator candidate passes schema/evidence/style and exact-normalized rejection checks. - Complementary access path: fresh semantic comparison of the actual full candidate against persisted user rejection records. - Independence boundary: separate stateless request with dedicated review instructions and no generator hidden reasoning/history; source of correction is user record, not a generator self-report. - Who acts on findings: the provider&#39;s repair loop withholds the candidate, requests revision and eventually substitutes a traceable non-interpretive fallback.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective capability-adaptation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These adjust current task content, context and service availability. They do not develop future environment/capability options and return an adaptation decision that changes the harness&#39;s present repertoire. Research/benchmark plans are adjacent. - Evidence: domain/context-builder.ts; domain/preferences.ts; server/providers/real-agent.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; docs/product-harness.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity or ultimate-policy decision/return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static identity/permissions and user authority over task/content/save do not supply ultimate-policy arbitration. No identity-level issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate decision owner and returned as a new governing policy. - Evidence: prompts/agent.ts; domain/policy.ts; domain/reducer.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; docs/product-harness.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8c0178fd561849a1d61ba41f63780227cb755b15/assessments/br/bread-companion.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8c0178fd561849a1d61ba41f63780227cb755b15\">8c0178f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T22:54:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jirathip-dev/herdsman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">herdsman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jirathip-dev/herdsman/tree/efd970e817c2021439c5415739e695f4af119b1d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision efd970e817c2021439c5415739e695f4af119b1d\">efd970e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: configured coding agent lanes implement and repair requested changes; separately tasked review agents exercise real task-specific judgment on code/tests. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new issue requirements, unknown codebase state, implementation errors, test failures and review findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which code and tests to change, what fixes to make and how to respond with evidence to reviewer findings within the owner-authorized issue. - Decision owner: model-based implementer and, for their bounded verification output, reviewer roles launched under the fleet pipeline; the deterministic drive merely chooses when to launch the next stage. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: herdr worktree/pane spawn, issue brief, role prompts, profile hooks, PR cache, head-pinned verdicts and event log. - Closure path: issue queued → implementer writes/tests/pushes change → reviewer evaluates exact head → FAIL returns findings to fix lane or PASS and green CI lead through merge gate → code reaches integration branch. - Boundary reachability: drive and timers launch the roles by supported fleet settings, and the named default pipeline wires their outputs back to future steps; this is not a unit-test-only role. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: code edits, targeted tests and adversarial judgments require meaningful agent choices; herdsman&#39;s hard transition and merge checks are constructor enforcement, not additional autonomous S1 judgment. - Evidence: role instructions, spawn source, pipeline. - Basis: documented supported operation plus pinned role and spawn wiring. - Confidence: high for model role construction, bounded for outcomes without a provider-backed run in this review. - Caveats: no agent executes without an installed, authenticated external harness and herdr; no unattended intelligence is attributed to the Rust scheduler itself.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: first-party conflict preview, serial integration gate and isolated lane worktrees attenuate specific interference among concurrently coding issue units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two issue agents editing overlapping files or branches cause rejected merges, overwrite/ordering hazards and repeated updates against the integration branch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: detect file-level collisions with git merge-tree, show the specific conflicting paths, order ready PRs, enforce required serial groups and return the losing lane to update its branch before another reviewed merge. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic overlap, drive, gate and serial-group rules; no model has a standing right to settle portfolio-wide branch conflicts by changing these policies. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated worktrees, cached head-pair collision preview, HEADS-UP prompts, exact-head verdicts, serial gate and update rounds with bounded retry. - Closure path: conflicting concurrent heads → warning/serial ordering and first merge → implementer of later head receives update instruction and incorporates integration → fresh reviewer verdict and CI → next eligible merge. - Boundary reachability: the installed drive calls overlap preview before action ordering, and the supported conflict/behind path actively spawns an update role. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concrete cross-issue contention is treated, but collision detection and merge precedence are coded invariants, not an autonomous S2 decision owner; this is C. - Distinct S1 units: independently running implementer lanes for different issue worktrees in the same configured fleet. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping file edits and stale branch ancestry can make otherwise valid S1 output conflict at integration. - Attenuating coordination relation: file-level preview and first-ready ordering, strict serial marking, integration update and mandatory fresh review. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: later implementer gets exact conflicting paths/updated integration instructions, edits its work and pushes a changed head for another review. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation recognizes actual write-set/merge interference between independent S1 agents and closes that conflict through a change in what the later agent does. - Evidence: overlap source, drive conflicts and ordering, lane conflict/update contract. - Basis: directly implemented collision-specific runtime path and prompt feedback. - Confidence: high for structural conflict attenuation; no claim of perfect prediction or autonomous negotiation. - Caveats: an early heads-up is advisory and does not lock all overlapping files; a first reviewed merge may still force an update round.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: the human fleet owner sees the live issue/lane/host portfolio and can pause or resume a fleet, intervene in blocked work and decide current resource and merge envelopes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: excessive concurrent lanes, host pressure, stalled fixes, owner questions and unwanted work on active issues. - Decisive decision or feedback right: use the phone board/CLI to pause or resume, move/park an issue, answer owner asks, extend review rounds or grant the narrow owner override without bypassing exact-head review and CI. - Decision owner: fleet owner/operator. drive computes the next mechanically valid transition, but does not set purpose/priorities as an AI metasystem. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live status, board and need-you cards, logged pause/decision events, queue moves, host admission and owner-only intervention controls. - Closure path: operator inspects current state → changes fleet pause/queue/decision or permitted round limit → next drive/kick reads event state and changes or withholds operational lane work. - Boundary reachability: pause, resume, ask, answer and board actions are shipped CLI/server paths and drive reads the same event log, not aspirational UI only. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the human selects priorities and discretionary intervention; programmed drive and resource thresholds do not upgrade to S3 A. - Whole-system current view: board shows queue, pipeline, lanes, host load, waiting and needs-you across a fleet. - Current-control decision scope: current issue order, which work waits or proceeds, review rounds, host/cost envelope and capacity within configured policy. - Evidence: owner board and drive behavior, live host control, pause/answer implementation. - Basis: explicit human command path and recorded return to scheduler. - Confidence: high for supported owner mode; model-directed supervisor not established. - Caveats: UI requires a running server/paired device; same control also exists through CLI.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independent, adversarial reviewer agents inspect implementation code and objective acceptance evidence outside the implementer&#39;s self-report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible but incorrect implementation claims, tests proving only mocks, leaked secrets and unreviewed scope changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: read the actual diff and criteria, independently rerun tests and return a head-specific PASS/FAIL with blocking findings that the implementer must address. - Decision owner: a separately launched read-only reviewer model in a distinct lane and role/profile; verdict and CI checks merely enforce validity and preserve its findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated reviewer worktree, read-only pre-tool hook, verdict.md with reviewed 40-hex head and tests checked, exact-head gate and fresh check-round spawn. - Closure path: implementation PR head → reviewer inspects raw code/tests/criteria independently → head-pinned verdict and findings → implementer fix or approved integration → new head invalidates prior verdict and is independently reviewed again. - Boundary reachability: default and custom pipelines include independent check stages, distinct from build, with verified role constraints and drive spawns on pushed heads. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adversarial technical judgment belongs to a distinct model role that can reject claims, not a boolean test wrapper or the implementer&#39;s own confirmation. - Claim being audited: implementation actually meets issue acceptance criteria and tests/PR description prove it. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer diff, test report and PR claim. - Complementary access path: independent checkout/diff, direct tests and head evidence against the original issue brief. - Independence boundary: reviewer role is prohibited from writing code, commits, pushes and PR comments, uses its own lane and verifies the actual current head; the same external harness vendor may still provide both models. - Who acts on findings: the subsequent implementer fix lane; herdsman&#39;s event/gate machine blocks merge until fresh PASS and green CI. - Evidence: reviewer role, review stage isolation, verdict source. - Basis: mandatory shipped review workflow and access separation. - Confidence: high for implemented audit path; reviewer accuracy not measured here. - Caveats: separate role/worktree provides process and tool isolation, not external institutional independence; no live paid provider review was performed in this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective outside-environment interpretation and return into enduring fleet capability changes was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new coding models, repository ecosystems, infrastructure economics and future workloads might eventually require adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no internal future-environment option selection and promotion right evidenced; host choices and code paths are bounded to present work/load. - Decision owner: not established for a separate S4 loop; fleet configuration and infrastructure upgrade choices remain with humans. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: host-history sampling, cost ceiling, doctor, backlog, context prompts and issue queue. - Closure path: observed present host pressure can deny a lane or start a configured cloud overflow host, but no projected external future becomes a selected enduring fleet strategy returned to implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: capacity observation is near-term S3/operational admission, and repository improvement by contributors is outside the installed fleet recursion. - Evidence: host placement and budget, architecture and scope. - Basis: bounded negative source review of shipped operating modes. - Confidence: medium-high for missing S4 closure in the reviewed modes. - Caveats: future model/host choice by the operator is possible but not credited as installed autonomous S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party loop was established that brings an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue to legitimate authority and returns the binding result to govern the fleet as a coherent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing the fleet&#39;s ultimate mission, reconciling external future adaptation with current obligations, or revising the constitutional distribution of authority could require such a loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the owner may edit fleet.toml and decide scoped owner asks, but no shipped identity-level agenda or decision-return path was evidenced beyond those task and administration actions. - Decision owner: not established for S5 at the declared fleet recursion; ultimate responsibility remains with the human owner outside the demonstrated herdsman function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: owner/routine ask classifier, OWNERTOPIC refusal, no-default owner answer, protected branches, command hooks, limit settings and fleet configuration. - Closure path: owner decisions are returned to individual tasks and settings bind future runs, but there is no demonstrated loop from an organization-level identity dispute through deliberation to a revised ultimate charter applied by the fleet. - Boundary reachability: task-level owner asks and configuration commands are shipped; their existence is not by itself S5 closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: approval for secrets, cost or scope during a single implementation may be important safety governance without constituting identity/policy-level VSM S5. - Evidence: owner/routine decision contract, ask/answer code, fleet policy and gate. - Basis: bounded negative first-party review, distinguishing task authorization from ultimate-policy closure. - Confidence: medium-high for this scoped absence; an independently organized human governance layer is not ruled out. - Caveats: this does not deny that an operator can govern or change herdsman outside the built fleet workflow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2500cd237846b78303b459e41ab53d20a64b0f13/assessments/he/herdsman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2500cd237846b78303b459e41ab53d20a64b0f13\">2500cd2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T17:39:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Beyon-Digital/control-plane\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Beyon Control Plane</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Beyon-Digital/control-plane/tree/9dbe4012bdf8b2d2ee333402912b408aadd35e6d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9dbe4012bdf8b2d2ee333402912b408aadd35e6d\">9dbe401 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: provider coding agents work on repo tasks through native worker session and tool/result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code, compilation failures, tool restrictions, interrupted provider processes and changing workspace state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each configured provider agent chooses coding actions and follow-up responses in its provider session, limited by its own approval policy and worker exposure; the first-party worker accepts, persists and reports concrete execution events. - Decision owner: model-backed Codex/Claude/Kiro agent; external provider inference is a runtime dependency, while Beyon owns the adapter, execution and feedback boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider stdio/sidecar adapters, scoped session creation, event stream, workspace tools, idempotency receipts, device authorization and abort. - Closure path: prompt is accepted by worker → provider agent works with permitted tools → worker persists events and file effects → operator/orchestrator reads outcome and can issue follow-up or cancellation. - Boundary reachability: README documents installed providers and worker start; orchestrator&#39;s concrete createsession and promptsession routes call authenticated v1/sessions endpoints. The provider must separately be installed/authenticated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the provider agent selects how to do coding work rather than simply replaying deterministic scripts; host safety and networking alone are not the autonomous decision. - Evidence: run and provider boundary, provider adapters, orchestrator session calls. - Basis: source and supported deployment documentation; no live provider inference run. - Confidence: high for reachability; real account/provider integration is not independently proven. - Caveats: the external agents&#39; internal organizational functions are not copied into first-party Beyon ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidenced inter-S1 conflict-specific reciprocal attenuation loop between independently acting coding agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent sessions could edit shared files or compete for worker resources, but the reviewed implementation does not show a first-party overlap analyzer and corrective return to the competing operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the orchestrator&#39;s tool calls are sequential and worker policies bound sessions/concurrency; neither explicitly identifies contested S1 obligations and reconciles them with collision feedback. - Decision owner: no distinct inter-S1 coordination owner established; session limits are deterministic worker enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sequential model calls, per-account isolation, role grants, session ownership, worker quotas, Git conditional saves and separate worktrees. - Closure path: requests are authenticated and serialized locally where required; a resource denial returns an error, not an evidenced negotiation/adjustment between multiple S1 agents. - Boundary reachability: concurrency and host/account controls are shipped but do not themselves prove inter-S1 regulatory coordination. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: having many sessions, a central orchestrator and an action cap is insufficient without identified actual collisions and a feedback relation specifically resolving those collisions. - Evidence: orchestrator tool loop, worker controls, workspace operations. - Basis: bounded first-party negative inspection. - Confidence: medium; a model might manually notice a conflict, but a reproducible supported collision workflow is not demonstrated. - Caveats: explicit separate worktrees reduce collision probability; they do not automatically constitute S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: a client-side main orchestrator can inspect the present portfolio of installed agents, active sessions, progress and host account capabilities, and direct current coding work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tasks accepted but not completed, unavailable providers, failed/uncertain sessions, unexpected file effects, new user priorities and permission changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: when management is enabled, the main model can select a provider, create sessions, prompt them and interrupt a live run after querying hostinfo, listsessions, sessionevents, workspacediff and other evidence. Human retains scope and approval rights. - Decision owner: separate model-backed first-party OrchestratorEngine for operational session-portfolio decisions; human owner supplies binding agent-management permission, host/account selection and provider/tool approvals. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoped API client, model tool catalog/dispatcher allowlist, per-turn 10-round/40-call budget, user toggle, persistent worker session events and host access checks. - Closure path: current host/session/workspace evidence → orchestrator chooses create/prompt/interrupt or wait/inspect → worker accepts/rejects → event states return to subsequent model judgment and user UI. - Boundary reachability: OrchestratorEngine.run calls configured model inference and OrchestratorTools.execute implements actual endpoints, available from the native Orchestrator screen; read-only mode removes management calls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current portfolio management is an explicit model decision layer distinct from a worker&#39;s deterministic dispatch; notation includes (P) because the user holds the management enablement and unapproved tool authority. - Whole-system current view: worker host/provider discovery, per-session states/events, files and Git state across the connected account. - Current-control decision scope: creation, follow-up prompting, interruption and current inspection; not identity policy, user consent overrides or provider permission approval. - Evidence: executable tool catalog and dispatch, orchestrator limits and scope. - Basis: first-party model loop and scoped live API wiring; not a successful real model-provider performance claim. - Confidence: medium-high for implemented mode, with uncertainty about account entitlement and real deployment operation. - Caveats: without a connected worker or with management disabled, this S3 agent&#39;s action right is absent; these modes are not silently credited with full orchestration.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: provide evidence independent of coding-agent prose about actual session acceptance/completion, worker state and Git working-tree differences, enabling a corrective decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a coding agent can claim a task is done when a prompt was only accepted or its changes are missing; client state can be stale after disconnect/restart. - Decisive decision or feedback right: orchestrator/human can query durable worker sessionevents, listsessions and raw workspacediff, compare to the coding agent&#39;s story and then change follow-up or interrupt; the alternative evidence path itself is deterministic rather than a dedicated autonomous auditor. - Decision owner: independent first-party worker event/actual-file evidence constructor (C); model orchestrator or human interprets it, but the code does not guarantee a distinct adversarial auditing-model verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable SQLite events and receipts, restart uncertain rather than success/replay, actual workspace Git diff endpoint, clear prompt acceptance semantics. - Closure path: provider report/accepted prompt → fresh worker events and raw Git diff challenge the claimed state → main orchestrator/user can send a correction or halt a session → next provider action and events reflect the adjustment. - Boundary reachability: model tool catalog includes these live read-only tools, backed by worker API and stored events; the docs explicitly instruct inspection rather than treating acceptance as completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these observations do not originate in the coding agent&#39;s natural-language assertion, yet no separate reviewer model automatically produces a verdict; hence a complementary constructor, not S3 A. - Claim being audited: the provider agent actually completed requested work and current workspace effects match its claimed progress. - Ordinary reporting path: provider messages, prompt-accepted notifications and local client conversation state. - Complementary access path: persisted host event cursor/status and independent Git filesystem diff read through the authenticated worker. - Independence boundary: coding agents can change workspace files and might manipulate their own outputs; the event store is separately maintained by the worker, but does not prove correctness, and actual Git diff alone cannot validate tests. - Who acts on findings: parent human or client-side orchestrator issues a new prompt, pauses, or explicitly declines completion claims. - Evidence: orchestrator independent read tools, durable events and restart semantics, worker store. - Basis: first-party separate observation and optional corrective return, not an observed independent semantic audit. - Confidence: medium: robust source separation, discretionary rather than mandatory corrective use. - Caveats: an orchestrator can choose not to inspect, and a diff can miss semantics; no guaranteed code-review safety threshold is inferred.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no packaged externally prospective scenario/option selection feeding durable changes to future operating capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future agent/provider availability, model eligibility, host topology and longer-term developer workflows may shift. - Decisive decision or feedback right: humans select current model profiles/endpoints, connect hosts and install workers; model discovery and account authorization are present-day capability choices, not a strategic outside-and-then return loop. - Decision owner: developer/operator configuration; no distinct first-party S4 intelligence decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model profiles and discovery, platform release/updater, host management, installation and verification guides. - Closure path: a user can switch a model or host and run the current orchestrator, but there is no shown future environment options assessment that binds a changed long-term operating capability through S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: discovering current models and installing signed updates are not VSM S4 merely because they concern technological change. - Evidence: model setup, architecture and update trust. - Basis: bounded negative review of shipped model/settings, current orchestrator and future release modes. - Confidence: medium-high at declared host/account recursion. - Caveats: developer roadmap and update mechanisms can evolve without proving a deployed prospective adaptation regulator.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no evidenced ultimate identity/purpose policy conflict adjudicated by a legitimate organizational authority and returned to runtime commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting device trust, human permission and long-term assistant identity may require parent policy, but runtime currently applies already configured controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: administrators enroll/revoke devices and grant roles; the user enables agent management, accepts provider permissions and can emergency-stop. These are scoped operational/security rights, not identity-policy adjudication. - Decision owner: outside human user/account administrator and project governance; no shipped distinct S5 decision loop with a charter-level return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TLS device identity, role/session ACL, certificate renewal, worker privilege boundaries, explicit trusted mode and emergency stop. - Closure path: permission changes apply to future API calls and revoke streams, without a witnessed foundational identity dispute being raised, decided and returned as ultimate policy across the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: revocation and human override are genuine safeguards but not sufficient S5 closure; no autonomous S5 is inferred from a secure product name or operator veto. - Evidence: trust model and deployment limits, human agent-management bounds. - Basis: bounded negative inspection of actual governance/permissions rather than contributor organization. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: multi-user administrators can govern security policies, but that is not an evidenced question-to-legitimate-S5-to-binding-operational-return loop at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8ba36072b4c05023dce5352227d9a0bad1958d31/assessments/be/beyon-control-plane.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8ba36072b4c05023dce5352227d9a0bad1958d31\">8ba3607 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:39:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/7ar-chat/harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">7ar harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/7ar-chat/harness/tree/89edbffde74f4aa2c7675dc07615f26ab76cae91\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 89edbffde74f4aa2c7675dc07615f26ab76cae91\">89edbff &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: a model-directed run selects and uses tools to produce the requested task outcome in a sandbox/host session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: diverse coding/information requests, filesystem and tool observations, failures, model responses and approval boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select tool calls, interpret results, continue/replan, produce task response or a code change/PR. - Decision owner: the configured live LLM-backed agent in each session or factory task; delegated agents own their bounded subtask choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ai/agent.Loop, sandbox adapters, tool registry, steering, durable events, approvals and per-task policy/budget hooks. - Closure path: model action → sandbox/tool result and event → next model turn or task conclusion; factory end hook records state, may request follow-up PR work. - Boundary reachability: the CLI and embedded/web hosts compose the loop; FactoryFeature installs a spawner and task dispatcher in the supported FACTORY=1 mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses the task work; queueing, policies, tool execution and approvals constrain but do not select the operational response. - Evidence: README quick start, single loop architecture, factory spawner and runtime hook. - Basis: structural; source paths and scripted tests, no live external model result claimed. - Confidence: high for first-party ownership/wiring. - Caveats: local backend does not itself isolate the host; a model key and applicable runtime configuration are required for real inference.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate conflicts among distinct task agents that share repository integration, dependencies and constrained execution capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing coding sessions/branches may conflict with the shared base, dependent tasks may start before prerequisites, and concurrent jobs may contend for quotas. - Decisive decision or feedback right: gate dependent task release, detect CI/merge conflicts, return actionable rebase/test feedback and defer incompatible integration; a whole-inter-S1 autonomous coordinator is not established. - Decision owner: deterministic factory task/forge mechanisms for admission and conflict detection; the receiving task agent decides its own fix, while the human/task policy controls merge permission. A function-specific constructor path exists, not a demonstrated autonomous S2 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: conditional task-state transitions, DependsOn release, MaxConcurrent, per-PR Mergeable/CI checks and MaxFixRounds feedback loop. - Closure path: dependency completion releases the waiting S1; an integration conflict or failing CI leaves the PR unmerged and passes feedback to the producing agent, which can rebase/fix and publish a new head for re-evaluation. - Boundary reachability: FactoryFeature composes tasks.Service and forge.Loop; these are invoked by the supported factory mode, not generic interfaces alone. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a repo-level S1 interaction is specifically regulated, but the decisions to defer/settle are mechanical or owner-governed; assigning model discretion to a named coordinator would overread the code. - Distinct S1 units: separate factory task agent sessions pursuing changes against one project repository, including bounded delegated sessions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: changes originating in different cells can invalidate a common base or require an ordering relation, and unchecked parallel execution can exceed current shared limits. - Attenuating coordination relation: dependency blocking/release and conflict-aware PR integration with CI/result/merge-state feedback, rather than unguarded simultaneous publication. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: ciFailed or conflictFeedback tells the same task agent to inspect, rebase/fix, test and reopen the PR; stored task state and checks are consulted before the next release or merge. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness concerns actual cross-cell interference at shared repository integration and prerequisite constraints; report collection, a tool queue or generic delegatemany alone is not being counted as S2. - Evidence: dependency state and release, CI and conflict feedback/resume, delegation isolation/limits. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; inter-S1 interference/closure is explicit, agent-owned system-wide coordination is not. - Caveats: the factory documents fake-host end-to-end coverage, not verified production-scale contention resolution.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: whole-current oversight and interventions over an installed user&#39;s/project&#39;s agent sessions in supported team mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: blocked approvals, stuck/failing sessions, priority of attention and conflicting active work commitments visible across sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which session to continue, steer, approve/deny, cancel or start, after an all-visible-session view. - Decision owner: the legitimate authenticated user/project member authorizes risky acts; the supervisor agent observes and recommends but does not independently execute these acts without user confirmation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listsessions, readsession, watcher, supervised session tools, tool approval gate, membership access checks, audit and event feed. - Closure path: user examines the supervisor&#39;s system-wide status/readout → authorizes its concrete action → sessionsend/approve/cancel/spawn changes an ongoing run, its permission hold or the current work inventory; audit records the decision. - Boundary reachability: harness-web -team installs the first-party app/product Supervisor; a watcher can proactively surface exceptions, and the user can converse with the supervisor in its dedicated thread. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-based attention triage is present, but every material intervention tool is explicitly registered as risky and requires the human&#39;s confirmation in this mode; runtime budgets/dispatch alone are not S3 authority. - Whole-system current view: overviewFor and listSessions aggregate accessible personal/project sessions with status, project, creator, current wait and update time, augmented by readsession context. - Current-control decision scope: human-approved correction/termination, unblock and allocation of new session work across the user&#39;s visible installed operation; this is not a claim over unrelated tenant organizations. - Evidence: supervisor contract and risky approvals, whole-current list and acting tools, team settings. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the explicitly enabled human-owned mode. - Caveats: FACTORY=1 and -team are opt-in; a standalone one-session CLI has no asserted S3 loop. Factory queue/scheduler is not counted as autonomous S3.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the coding agent&#39;s completion claim before integration, using a direct code-host diff and a configured different reviewer model. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a coding agent may submit defective, insecure or task-incomplete changes while its own summary or CI gives an incomplete account. - Decisive decision or feedback right: emit approve/reject and findings on the exact PR; block merge or return fix feedback to the producing task. - Decision owner: a separately configured FACTORYREVIEWMODEL with explicit different-model enforcement for that task; mechanical security/test checks are supporting guards. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: forge direct PR diff/comments, mechanical protected-path/secrets/injection checks, CI-success requirement, model review rubric, head-specific recorded verdict and guarded merge. - Closure path: CI-ready PR → independent diff/reviewer judgment → forge.Loop.gate posts a verdict → approval allows guarded merge (only if AutoMerge) or findings resume/block the authoring S1, affecting the next attempt. - Boundary reachability: FactoryFeature installs the review gate when Forge is configured and review is not disabled; setting a distinct ReviewModel explicitly requires reviewer/author model inequality. This is a supported installed mode, not a borrowed external organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the configured reviewer model judges a code-host artifact without relying on the producing agent&#39;s report; default fallback to the same model is explicitly weaker and not credited as independent. - Claim being audited: the authoring factory agent&#39;s PR actually satisfies its task and is safe to integrate. - Ordinary reporting path: the author/agent task result and PR/CI status used by the normal factory work loop. - Complementary access path: the gate fetches actual PR patch/comments from GitHub, runs independent static checks and asks a distinct model to inspect the diff. - Independence boundary: distinct configured model checked by RequireDifferentModel, outside the producer&#39;s agent conversation; only this mode is counted, not the default same-model fallback. - Who acts on findings: factory PR loop withholds merging, publishes feedback and restarts the authoring task, or blocks for a human; successful verdict remains SHA-specific. - Evidence: reviewer distinct-model and direct-diff checks, gate feedback and guarded merge, factory feature review wiring, documented same-model caveat and setup limitation. - Basis: structural, conditional on the documented distinct-reviewer mode. - Confidence: medium; strongly wired source path, no actual independent live provider review was executed for this assessment. - Caveats: NoReview disables the gate; without a separate reviewer model the default self-review explicitly warns that it is not independent. Project CI and GitHub are complementary evidence sources, not ownership of the audit judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated installed decision loop that interprets external future change and selects/returns a prospective capability adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new future tasks, repo practices, provider conditions and failures can require changed capabilities; present modes primarily address active or past tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S4-specific prospective adaptation judgment with a returned change in the installed operating model was found. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional factory lessons distillation and recall, memory, project configuration, cron triggers, coding benchmarks and feature extensibility. - Closure path: failed/completed task lessons can be recalled in later prompts; this changes operational context but does not select an external-and-future option and install a revised organizational capability under S4 control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-generated lessons and new task prompts are retrospective operating memory, not a demonstrated strategic environmental scan/adaptation closure. - Evidence: lessons feature, distillation/recall, docs factory/bench boundary. - Basis: bounded negative-state structural review. - Confidence: medium-high for shipped mode. - Caveats: an external project owner can redesign/deploy the system; contributor development is outside this deployed recursion and does not imply a shipped S4 parent loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: a legitimate human project owner settles and changes the installed project&#39;s identity, membership authority and binding operating boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project/repository identity changes, member access conflicts and the limits under which assistant or factory agents may act. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create/update the project, change its repository/default branch and assign/revoke member roles; maintain at least one owner. - Decision owner: authenticated human project owner (and separately system admin for account approval), never the task agent or deterministic role checker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: owner-only routes, persistent Project/Member store, role-based guards on API/supervisor operations, audit/feed, per-task policy and merge-permission enforcement. - Closure path: identity/membership/policy issue → project owner edits authoritative project/roles (or approves a new actor) → changes persist and are enforced on subsequent session access, supervisor acts, factory task/project use and integration permissions. - Boundary reachability: harness-web -team provides owner-gated project patch/member routes; membership changes immediately govern user and agent-action access in the same installed product. No upstream maintainer authority is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent may propose work and act with human approval, but ultimate project membership, repository identity and operating authority remain owner-governed; static policy checks enforce their decisions. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: which repository constitutes the project and who may authorize/perform work on behalf of its organization. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the project owner in team mode; no autonomous ultimate-policy mode established for a single CLI agent. - Return-to-operation path: owner-only PATCH /projects/{pid} or role assignment → stored project/role update → subsequent requireProject and server/agent tool checks recognize the new authority and branch/repo context. - Evidence: owner-only project/role changes, team membership and access model, factory policy/merge modes. - Basis: structural parent-governed team mode. - Confidence: medium; explicit authorization/return path, not a claim about the upstream OSS organization&#39;s policy process. - Caveats: ordinary tool approval or a single PR merge veto alone would not justify S5; the claimed right is the project-identity/member-authority loop.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8278b99a5810ef26bcf4000cc6213cb41cbc4149/assessments/7a/7ar-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8278b99a5810ef26bcf4000cc6213cb41cbc4149\">8278b99 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:46:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stoicdataguy/asktravel-agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AskTravel Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stoicdataguy/asktravel-agent-harness/tree/9204095f29720ba70cdd90c9ccd6d214cda36c72\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9204095f29720ba70cdd90c9ccd6d214cda36c72\">9204095 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce researched trip advice and structured itineraries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: destinations, preferences, incomplete data, live tool observations and invalid itinerary arguments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose searches/playbooks, interpret observations and select/revise the itinerary. - Decision owner: the instantiated travelagent model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangChain execution, first-party tool wrappers, Postgres, retry/compaction middleware and Pydantic validation. - Closure path: tool and validation observations return to the model; presentitinerary persists and displays the chosen plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves storage/search wrappers without discretionary travel synthesis. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/api/openaicompat.py; src/travelagent/tools/itinerary.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Bounded source assessment of the supported distribution; no live model or performance claim. - Boundary reachability: API lifespan constructs buildagent; the public chat router invokes that graph with user context.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No supplied regulation of a specific inter-S1 disturbance is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel tools, independent chats and background upkeep must not be equated with coordinated autonomous work cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: thread/checkpoint branching, user namespaces, parallel tool execution and idle scheduling. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/api/threadsync.py; src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; src/travelagent/tools/memory.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-service current resource/commitment decision loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider rate limits and background workload are handled as configured runtime constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: retry backoff, capped users per maintenance cycle, API state and database persistence. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/retry.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge the travel actor&#39;s persisted user-fact claims against raw user statements outside the ordinary chat-production path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misfiled interests/dislikes, contradicted or retracted saved facts and missing user-stated preferences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether a saved user claim needs add/update/delete/noop and propose the smallest source-supported correction. - Decision owner: the separately prompted structured memory-review model invoked by llmproposer; not applyplan&#39;s deterministic matcher. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: idle-chat discovery, checkpoint access, guarded store writes, source-quote checks, sensitive-fact exclusion, MAXOPS and memorylog. - Closure path: the reviewer proposes corrections; accepted operations update the shared fact store, and graph.systemprompt reloads that store on subsequent model calls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the reviewer the scheduler and quote checker cannot decide semantic reclassification or contradiction; they only constrain/apply its proposed judgment. - Evidence: src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; src/travelagent/tools/memory.py; src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; tests/testconsolidation.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: A narrowly scoped source-supported memory-claim audit, not travel-answer verification. Same provider is permitted; trust is limited by selected recent messages, saved-fact evidence, model error, quote matching and excluded sensitive fields. No measured audit effectiveness. - Claim being audited: persisted user facts written by ordinary savememory calls faithfully represent user preferences/statements. - Ordinary reporting path: the travel actor writes summarized facts through savememory and later consumes their prompt projection. - Complementary access path: consolidateuser reads HumanMessage content directly from checkpoints and compares it to the store in a fresh structured review context. - Independence boundary: the producing chat actor neither selects the idle review invocation nor supplies its ordinary narrative as sole evidence; separate reviewer prompt and raw user messages can challenge that actor&#39;s stored claim. Shared provider/store do not establish global independence or coverage. - Who acts on findings: guarded applyplan updates memory and logs before/after; subsequent travel calls reread corrected facts. - Boundary reachability: app.lifespan creates the nonstreaming proposer and runconsolidationloop when default memoryconsolidation is enabled; disabled mode intentionally has no audit run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environment-to-capability adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: live travel availability and changed user preferences are current-task/domain data. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: search tools, skill playbooks, memory consolidation and dynamic prompt refresh. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/prompts.py; src/travelagent/skills/init.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; docs/PLAN.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime ultimate-policy or identity decision-and-return path is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task preferences and data controls are not identity-level organizational disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system guidelines, sensitive-memory guard, settings and user export/delete endpoints. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/prompts.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5ae1eb582c8453ba6a02b1827749d624be95ba28/assessments/as/asktravel-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5ae1eb582c8453ba6a02b1827749d624be95ba28\">5ae1eb5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"3\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T16:44:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eust-w/opengeniemaster\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenGenieMaster</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>3</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eust-w/opengeniemaster/tree/8f6dab22a54a318bf8afa221269a6f9c00fa860c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8f6dab22a54a318bf8afa221269a6f9c00fa860c\">8f6dab2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: primary embodied domain work (e.g. inspect/restock, retrieve, clean, handoff) by agent-guided robot work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user task variation, unavailable/unknown objects, visual occlusion, robot capability limits, unexpected failures, interruptions and contradictory task outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the in-process Claude coding agent chooses task decomposition, relevant tools, guidance, execution/recovery/handoff and whether to retry, based on live task context and returned observations. - Decision owner: ClaudeAgent in the optional first-party ogm agent mode; individual robot policy owns lower-level execution, not the higher-level coding-agent choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolRegistry, EEP schema, Harness, domain templates, adapters, admission interlocks, task store, completion criteria, typed failure/recovery hints, bounded turn count. - Closure path: goal → model tool decision → Harness.execute/robot → observation, checked outcome and persisted continuity → next model turn or terminal response. - Boundary reachability: documented ogm agent command constructs ClaudeAgent with first-party operator prompt and directly wired EEP tools; optional Anthropic SDK/API availability is an execution prerequisite. It is not necessary to invent an agent in application code. Offline fake-client tests demonstrate tool-loop wiring, not actual paid-model behaviour. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing ClaudeAgent leaves ScriptedOrchestrator, which can follow fixed recovery rules but does not retain the agent&#39;s open-ended choice of guidance and recovery under novel user contexts. S1 A belongs narrowly to the shipped optional Claude mode; the deterministic demo does not itself establish A. - Evidence: Claude tool loop, operator instructions, EEP handlers, execution/feedback, CLI agent entrypoint. - Basis: first-party optional agent tool loop with consequential environment feedback. - Confidence: high for code-level reachability, moderate for deployed agent outcomes (no provider E2E run). - Caveats: robot simulator is semantic; physical policy autonomy and real-world safety are not verified; provider model behaviour was not exercised.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: dampen conflicts between distinct embodied S1 work cells over capacity, shared work-item affinity and interrupted handoffs. - Distinct S1 units: two or more separately assigned robot/policy work cells with their own capability and physical state in a fleet; not two names for the same Claude agent. - Inter-S1 disturbance: assigning one busy robot simultaneously, transferring an unfinished pick–carry–place group while the first robot still holds its object, running dependent steps before prerequisites, or handing off to an incapable robot. - Attenuating coordination relation: Harness.dispatch excludes busy/stopped units, respects group affinity and dependencies; updatetask(assign/handoff) mediates exceptional reassignment. The agent&#39;s operating prompt directs failure-driven handoff and deliberate retry/wait/stop decisions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: assignment and handoff update persistent TaskStore; the next eeptaskget / eepdispatch / execution uses the new robot, group and dependency state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the code explicitly detects the inter-robot work-item/capacity conflict and blocks/moves conflicting assignments, including held-object restart semantics; a simple shared task list would not do so. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent incompatible robot assignments and unstable cross-robot handoff in shared work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the autonomous Claude agent can choose to wait, hand off/reassign, force-restart affected work items, or route around failures based on robot feedback; deterministic dispatch enforces ordinary constraints but does not itself own discretionary S2 judgment. - Decision owner: ClaudeAgent for exception coordination in the supported multi-robot mode; deterministic Harness for bound-preserving enforcement. Routine default dispatch alone would support only constructor/enforcement, not A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: busy-robot set, capability filtering, group binding, held-object tracking, dependency graph, continuity persistence, readiness checks. - Closure path: two robot work cells create assignment/holding conflict → feedback/continuity visible through EEP → agent chooses handoff/retry/alternate robot → guard updates whole work item → subsequent dispatch/execution observes new ownership. - Boundary reachability: multi-robot scenarios and eeptaskupdate handoff are first-party tools available directly to ClaudeAgent; no custom coordinator is required in that mode. S2 A does not claim the scripted dispatcher has autonomous organizational discretion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the full exception-response choice can be made by the tool-using model; deterministic locks/guards are supporting regulators. Removing the model would retain fixed handoff mechanics but not its context-sensitive coordination discretion. - Evidence: operating prompt, dispatch and busy rules, handoff safeguards, continuity group assignment, handoff regression. - Basis: identifiable multi-S1 interference, conflict-specific guard, model-accessible discretionary recovery and returned assignment state. - Confidence: moderate: source and synthetic test wiring are clear; real multi-robot model behavior is untested. - Caveats: if physical robots are only passive actuators under one central actor, the organizational S2 interpretation narrows; standard simulated policy work cells still retain local execution environments and separate assignments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: provide a current whole-fleet task/priority/resource regulation path, beyond local per-step delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous active tasks competing for finite robot availability, differing priorities, stopped robots and unmet commitments. - Whole-system current view: eeptasklist lists all tasks, eepcapabilities lists fleet skills/status, Harness.busyrobots spans active, paused and interrupted tasks, and ScriptedOrchestrator.runready enumerates all active tasks. - Current-control decision scope: assign resources across all ready tasks, use task priority when selecting the next work item, stop/pause or restart commitments and intervene on assignment. Task priority is chosen at creation, not autonomously renegotiated by a dedicated whole-system governor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a developer/composed operator or future first-party agent policy must decide whole-system priorities, commitments and exceptions; the shipped scheduler executes preselected priorities and capacity rules. - Decision owner: no autonomous agent demonstrably assigned sustained whole-system S3 discretion in the standard operating instructions. The caller chooses priorities and when the scheduler runs; scripts enforce them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: priority-sorted task store, cross-task dispatch, runready highest-priority selection, stop/resume and event log. - Closure path: all-task view + priorities → scheduled fleet assignment/intervention → persistent task/robot state → next scheduling pass. The first-party constructor is real; an autonomous whole-system deciding/feedback actor must still be composed. - Boundary reachability: eeptasklist, priority at eeptaskcreate, cross-task eepdispatch and ScriptedOrchestrator.runready are shipped callable modes. No private benchmark oracle is needed. This is a function-specific S3 path, not mere generic tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scripted runready orders work by caller-supplied priority; Claude&#39;s documented ogm agent goal prompt serves one task and does not establish an autonomous sustained fleet-wide current-control review. Enforcement is not ownership. Human eeptaskupdate calls are local operations, not a distinct operationally closed parent S3 mode. - Evidence: all-task and fleet view, task priorities and dispatch, cross-task busy guard, scheduler runready, tool registry. - Basis: shipped S3-specific whole-current-task capacity/priority constructor with missing autonomous decisive S3 policy. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: no operator-governed (P) whole-system governance closure, budget bargaining, or accountable strategic priorities is demonstrated.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge the robot policy&#39;s own success report and the ordinary step-completion record through a separately callable, more direct evidence check. - Claim being audited: the physical action succeeded and its effect persists (e.g. an item really remains in the intended slot, not just policy-reported success). - Ordinary reporting path: robot adapter execute returns a policy success/outcome and observation; harness evaluates immediate criteria, records execution and updates task status. - Complementary access path: eepverify / recheckstep later calls criterion checks, optionally at close range, through the adapter&#39;s separate check operation; contradictory results mark prior execution records, convert succeeded steps to failed, and reopen completed tasks. - Independence boundary: the verification request and criterion signal are separate from the policy&#39;s initial self-reported success, and can overturn it in the shipped simulator. However physical HttpAdapter.check calls the same configured robot service (/check), with no guaranteed independent detector/operator/credential ownership; the model can also invoke the check itself. This is an explicit complementary constructor, not a demonstrated organizationally independent S3 auditor. - Who acts on findings: the harness reconciles continuity deterministically, and the coding agent can read discrepancies, recover or create new memory; a distinct audit owner/trigger/sampling authority is not supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false-positive policy completion, persistent-effect regressions and false negatives hidden in routine execution summaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independent auditor&#39;s selection of when/how to check and corrective judgment is not operationally assigned; the first-party alternative-evidence and contradiction-to-rework feedback path is implemented. - Decision owner: needs first-party-mode integration/developer composition for independent S3 authority; Harness.recheckstep owns deterministic reconciliation, not independent audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: criteria API, alternate camera/close-range flag, contradiction records, task reopening and offline regression tests. - Closure path: policy claim → later direct criterion check → contradiction/reopening and marked evidence → S1 rework and future reflection. Distinct auditor authority is the missing component for A. - Boundary reachability: eepverify is exposed to the built-in Claude and MCP agents and Harness.recheckstep is shipped in the runtime; this is more than a test-only audit, though real detector independence is not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a same-loop verifier can provide valuable extra evidence but does not prove a separately controlled independent audit; C recognizes the material constructor without fabricating A. - Evidence: recheck and task reopening, robot adapter direct check, tool eepverify, regression showing overturned completion, criteria. - Basis: separate criterion and corrective-return path, conditional independence at the evidence level, missing independent audit ownership for A. - Confidence: moderate; stronger simulation path than unverified real backend. - Caveats: same backend may correlate failures; normal immediate completion-criterion checks alone would not suffice for S3.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: expose a capability-adaptation path using observed environmental difficulty and prospective targeted rehearsal, then return learned alternative guidance/coordination into subsequent work. - External distinction: repeated physical-world failures in particular robot/model/skill/object contexts, e.g. occluded targets, look-alike stock, changed item placements and slow handling, compared across execution records and domain catalog. - Future / prospective distinction: capability-gap severity and planned idle-time practice for troublesome contexts before the next customer/work task; this is an opportunity to develop/rehearse future response capacity, not just retry the current failed step. - Adaptation option generated: capabilitygaps groups recent failures, relative attempts and slowness; planpractice generates low-priority repeated pick/return tasks; RuleReflector or optional LLMReflector mines successful recoveries and contradictions into contextual patch instructions. - Path back into current capability / S3: practice runs produce execution records → evidence-checked SharedAdmission admits or rejects guidance/recovery/criteria/coordination patches → GuidanceBuilder and chooserobot apply relevant memory on later dispatch/execution; weak non-safety memory can be retired based on outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: foreseeable performance deterioration or new capability demands in observed external object/robot contexts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: whether and when to run future-oriented practice, what capacity to spend, which gap to prioritize, and whether to make this a standing adaptation program. eepcapabilitygaps(schedulepractice=true) is callable, but no automatic idle/foresight owner and no sustained whole-system adaptation conversation with S3 are assigned in the standard runtime. - Decision owner: caller/developer must compose the autonomous S4 agenda and authority; deterministic gap ranking/practice creation and evidence-gated memory support do not by themselves own prospective choice. An optional Claude agent could call the constructor, but the shipped operator prompt lacks a recurring future-environment scan/idle planning loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution record history, contextual failure aggregation, low-priority practice workflow, rule/LLM reflection, schema/evidence/safety admission, versioned long-term memory and replay tests. - Closure path: environmental difficulty → exposed gap and practice option → caller-scheduled rehearsal → feedback/reflection/admitted patch → changed future guidance/robot preference. The function-specific construction path exists, but autonomous S4 initiative remains to be composed. - Boundary reachability: eepcapabilitygaps with schedulepractice and memory tools are actually in the shipped tool registry, not just a README roadmap; rule reflection and admitted patches run in standard simulation and the MCP mode. Physical extrapolation not claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a reflective memory loop alone is not S4. The distinct prospective practice option and future capability return make this more than generic memory, while lack of a scheduled autonomous environmental/adaptation governor keeps it at C. - Evidence: capability-gap/practice construction, tool exposure, reflection, admission safety/evidence, memory application, architecture memory path. - Basis: first-party prospective gap/practice-to-later-capability constructor; no complete autonomous S4 decision loop or parent mode. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: gaps are mined from internal execution records of externally situated work, not broad market/strategy sensing. There is no robot-weight self-training; flywheel.py stops at dataset export.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: would decide or revise ultimate identity/mission and balance current robot operations against the future adaptation program. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unresolved mission/safety/strategic-policy tensions beyond routine robot-task control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party runtime path accepts a genuinely ultimate-policy question, routes it to legitimate authority, records an authoritative identity decision and returns it to govern subsequent operation. - Decision owner: none evidenced for an active S5 loop; hard-coded safety constraints and user task goals do not constitute an identity governor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: domain safety rules, SharedAdmission rejection of unsafe guidance/memory, robot stop/resume, current-model prompt, human teleoperation example, repo contributor governance. - Closure path: not established at ultimate-policy level; operational stop/resume and evidence-based memory patches affect tasks or safety settings rather than deciding identity or arbitrating S3–S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent cannot revise ultimate mission under a dedicated first-party process, and no first-party parent institution/human S5 escalation-return path is supplied. Accepting task goals and protecting immutable safety flags are ordinary operating constraints. - Evidence: operator prompt/safety, admission rules, memory admission cannot weaken safety, robot stop/resume, human backend, research vs repo identity note. - Basis: bounded negative conclusion for reviewed runtime rather than universal impossibility. - Confidence: moderate-high within shipped modes. - Caveats: a real deployment may have human/organizational identity governance outside this repository, but that external authority cannot be inferred here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1c021299b02811b5e34a4b01833cdba494fca0b3/assessments/op/opengeniemaster.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1c021299b02811b5e34a4b01833cdba494fca0b3\">1c02129 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T14:37:34+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/uniac-ai/AgentOrg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentOrg</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/uniac-ai/AgentOrg/tree/25e116cb719989ce568b272016b1936668efbf5f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 25e116cb719989ce568b272016b1936668efbf5f\">25e116c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: attached autonomous agent sessions carry out locally variable project work and return artifacts/results as Work submissions within their declared position. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing project requirements, source/tool results, work feedback and manager requests that demand operational judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select task actions and revise work within the Actor&#39;s purpose, then submit a result and respond to returned review or ask answers. - Decision owner: attached Claude Code/Codex or other autonomous agent session in the documented first-party MCP operating mode; operator supplies initial position/credentials and task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Hub Actor registry, MCP adapter, Workspace records, audit store and session hooks, Provider provisioning (optional). - Closure path: agent receives assigned Work/attention, acts in its own project runtime, records progress and submits; requester reviews or changes assignment, after which the session receives and acts on returned state. - Boundary reachability: the distributed MCP adapter and bundled skill explicitly attach supported agent sessions and supply the Work/attention/submit path. This is a wired operating mode, not merely an open-ended API a developer might someday connect. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: cognition and project actions are in the attached external agent, credited as an actor only because the installed mode explicitly wires its decision/action/report loop; Hub persistence alone is not S1 autonomy. - Evidence: MCP agent client/attachment, Work bindings, agent Work protocol. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Hub alone creates no model session and cannot guarantee external agent behavior; no paid provider runtime was executed in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent simultaneous operational Actors from destructively claiming the same shared resource while they do distinct Work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two active S1 positions can contend for one release lane, cluster or other shared named resource. - Decisive decision or feedback right: caller seeks/renews/releases a resource claim; Hub refuses another active holder and names the conflict; an agent must compose its own response, negotiation and later retry. - Decision owner: the holder/contender Actor selects a requested resource and intended follow-up; deterministic Hub claims enforce exclusive holding and expiry, but the standard distribution does not install an autonomous conflict-resolution policy for all Actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite claim store, lifetime/holder checks, authenticated Actor identity, conflict refusals and root override. - Closure path: the second claim returns a named-holder refusal; a custom agent policy or manager can subsequently wait, negotiate, change its work or retry after release/expiry, using the first-party claim path. - Boundary reachability: named claims are an explicit shipped Hub Tool available to attached Actors and the root, and the skill documents their shared-resource purpose. The autonomy/coordination response after refusal requires composition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concrete exclusive-claim regulation is first-party and function-specific, but its mandatory refusal/expiry judgment is deterministic and no universal autonomous coordination-closure actor is installed. This warrants constructor C rather than A. - Distinct S1 units: separately attached active agent positions in one Hub organization, each with its own Work and local task environment. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing claims for the same named shared lane or resource. - Attenuating coordination relation: exclusive holder check with conflict identity and explicit release/expiry. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: refusal tells the contender who holds the resource; custom agent/operator behavior can adapt task choice or retry once the claim ends. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: claims.ts enforces a specific shared-resource collision and returns a conflict, rather than merely transferring messages or exposing untyped storage. - Evidence: claim implementation, domain Tool catalog, bundled skill organization commands. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no mandatory agent-side claim discipline or automatic release notification/negotiation is established; the Hub&#39;s isolated conflict decision is not by itself evidence of agent S2 ownership.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current organization-wide or managerial-subtree commitments by assigning Work, responding to subordinate asks and accepting or returning submitted results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: reports may block, diverge from assigned work, need approvals or consume commitments that must be reviewed and reprioritized by a responsible manager. - Decisive decision or feedback right: see current direct-report Work/asks, choose ownership and goals, answer/escalate actionable asks, review a specific submission and return changes or accept it. - Decision owner: in the agent-manager mode, the attached manager&#39;s agent exercises this discretionary current-control right; in the distinct root CLI mode, the legitimate human root can make equivalent authoritative choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: team, actorview, Work and Forms records, role checks, attention/waiter delivery and stored revisions. - Closure path: manager observes report/ask/submission, chooses answer/reassignment or workreview verdict, Hub stores it, and the subordinate receives attention and updates/resubmits Work. Human root mode uses the same return path. - Boundary reachability: the standard-distribution agent skill directly instructs managers to inspect team state, assign/review subordinate Work and handle asks; the shipped root CLI can act as the canonical human root with the Hub&#39;s authenticated rights. Both modes use the same installed Work/attention path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in manager-agent mode, the agent selects which current intervention to make; deterministic record/authority checks enforce it. In root mode, the human is the decision owner. These are alternative modes, not concurrent owners of one decision. - Whole-system current view: manager&#39;s team and actorview show its reports&#39; Work state, current asks and review queue; canonical root can see the whole organization. - Current-control decision scope: assigning Work goals/owners, reviewing submitted commitments, answering/escalating blocking asks and changing subsequent report activity. - Evidence: team/attention projections, Workspace operation bindings, agent manager procedure, root CLI authority. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: not every connected Actor is a manager; an agent-specific actual review choice was not exercised with a provider. Human root control is specifically current-work supervision, not an S5 identity-policy claim.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no shipped independent complementary inspection of operational reality beyond ordinary work reporting and manager reviews is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workers can misreport completion, but a separate audit-specific counter-observation path is not provided by the Hub. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party independent raw-artifact audit judgment with corrective return is established. - Decision owner: managers review submissions through ordinary Work state; the root reads operation metadata, neither becomes a distinct packaged S3 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: append-only activity metadata, Work review verdicts, status/attention, Hub audit events and client context. - Closure path: review changes can return to a worker as S3 accountability, not an independently sourced complementary operational audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a root activity read observes tool-call metadata, while workreview is normal reporting/review; no separately deployed direct-evidence auditor is wired. - Evidence: activity and Work boundaries, audit metadata code, manager skill. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a connected organization&#39;s external agents can run tests or review code, but that independently configured audit is not a default Hub function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective environment/option-generation and return-to-current-capability loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external demand and capability changes may affect the organization but are not converted by a shipped S4 process into adaptation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct future-oriented model, option selection and S3 return path. - Decision owner: an agent/user may propose new work, but the Hub has no S4-specific owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Work backlog, reports/asks, status, provider catalog, generic messages and stored historical activity. - Closure path: no shipped external-prospecting option-to-implementation path; Work assignments remain present-task control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: storage and future-dated Work do not show externally grounded environmental modeling or adaptation. - Evidence: Tool catalog and projections, agent skill. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: organization-specific skills might implement prospective assessment; they are external to this reviewed standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: the Hub models organization membership/purpose and enforces role authority, but no first-party identity/ultimate-policy deliberation and authoritative return loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: creating/closing a position, credential revocation and action approvals are governed structural operations, not necessarily policy/identity tensions of the whole organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct workflow takes an ultimate-policy issue to legitimate authority, decides organizational ethos/policy and returns an enforceable new policy into operation. - Decision owner: human root chooses membership and routine configuration; agent managers can create authorized reports. Neither is evidenced as an S5-specific decision path merely from hierarchical titles or rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Actor registry, credentials, lifecycle checks, hierarchy, approval Forms and filtered interfaces. - Closure path: Actor create/close and approval answers change ordinary access/work; absent is an explicit identity-policy issue/decision/returned governance process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a root actor and purpose fields do not themselves prove the VSM S5 function; generic Forms allow many applications but do not compose ultimate policy. - Evidence: registry/lifecycle, Hub design authority, agent skill approval limits. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a particular organization may implement genuine parent S5 over these generic tools, but that case requires its own first-party operating contract and evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/448a5f4f4273aa7aaa5175f4e71e491562962a2d/assessments/ag/agentorg.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 448a5f4f4273aa7aaa5175f4e71e491562962a2d\">448a5f4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T23:02:10+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sgeos/pydromon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pydromon</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sgeos/pydromon/tree/65671672b812b1d4c88f8e7633fdd3a1e50bb5f0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 65671672b812b1d4c88f8e7633fdd3a1e50bb5f0\">6567167 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Configured session lines produce project work and exchange results through their first-party daemon composition. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User tasks, local workspace changes, messages, background runs and engine/tool failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose local tools, communications, work actions and whether to continue or end a turn. - Decision owner: The model-backed session-line actor; external Claude/Codex/Pi engines supply inference/tool-loop dependencies rather than autonomous Dromon metasystem judgments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party driver construction, tool exposure, mailbox/event log, wake scheduler, sandbox/settings composition and persisted engine sessions. - Closure path: Queued mail/wake → line prompt and configured engine → chosen tools/messages/work → recorded turn outcomes → later line turns. - Boundary reachability: The distributed daemon creates configured session lines with first-party Dromon tools and driver/engine wiring. The supported Claude/Codex-backed composition is executable without implementing a new harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model-backed line actors, the daemon transports messages and enforces policy but cannot choose meaningful project work. Dromon does not receive credit for unrelated engine features. - Evidence: [Scheduler][R1], [engine composition][R2], [daemon][R3], [line configuration][R4], [README][R5]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No live subscribed engine or paid model was run; engine independence and operational quality are not claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Declared resource leases and role-seat ownership regulate concrete shared-resource/write collisions among operational lines. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two active lines may try to use the same named project resource; role seats sharing a worktree must avoid competing writers and stale ownership transitions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A line chooses when to acquire/release a resource lease and can hand off or recover role ownership subject to observed eligibility. - Decision owner: Configured agent lines own invocation/release/handoff choices; the operator declares permitted resource names/role terms, and deterministic state validates exclusivity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Lease holder/expiry events, refusal with holder/expiration feedback, non-renewal/TTL, role owner invariants, engine access rebuilding and journaled handoff. - Closure path: Contender calls acquire → receives grant or current-holder/expiry refusal → adjusts later resource use; release/expiry enables another acquisition. Role handoff records a note, transfers authority, wakes the successor and rebuilds the former owner&#39;s access. - Boundary reachability: acquirelease/releaselease and seat ownership tools are supplied to running lines by Scheduler; project-config leases/roles enable the shipped path, not a custom conflict resolver. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The actors decide when to reserve/release/pass work; the log and guards enforce those decisions. This credits that bounded agent-mediated coordination mode, not generic messaging or the scheduler itself. - Evidence: [Lease tool and role tool implementations][R1], [role invariants/one-writer boundary][R6], [project enablement][R7], [line tool semantics][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Resource leases are cooperative: unmanaged external processes or ignored lease conventions are not forcibly excluded. Deterministic automatic failover is support, not autonomous discretion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: The project operator regulates the whole current set of session lines, role capacity, budgets and continuing commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Runaway autonomous turns/cost, held or failed lines, exhausted provider plans, conflicting current staffing and engine availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review project-wide state and choose line/role/budget/hibernation/rebind/interruption/release changes or answer a held line. - Decision owner: The authenticated local project operator, acting as the legitimate parent of the project run. No first-party autonomous whole-project controller is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Private operator socket, all-line status summaries, durable events, operator-attributed messages, hold notices/question queue, CLI commands and scheduler/engine enforcement. - Closure path: Whole-project status or held-line notice → operator current-control choice → daemon command/event → scheduler applies limits, ownership, model, hold or interruption changes → subsequent turns run under the returned decision. - Boundary reachability: Daemon.execute serves operator-authority status/settings/role/budget/configure/release/interrupt commands, and status includes every line. The first-party scheduler routes hold questions and operator answers back into later operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the operator leaves fixed limits and failover machinery, not discretionary whole-system resource/commitment revision. P describes the supplied supervised mode; enforcement does not become A or C. - Evidence: [Operator protocol dispatch/current status][R3], [holds and operator-answer scheduler][R1], [project limits/roles][R7], [role runtime updates][R6], [client protocol][R8]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The parent boundary is this local project, not the public OSS organization; operator socket access is an explicit trust assumption.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No installed complementary independent audit judgment-and-corrective-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect work-completion claims, stale notes, disclosure mistakes and engine-reporting errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separate audit-specific actor or constructor adjudicates operational truth and returns corrective control. - Decision owner: Lines report their work; runtime observes process/session/log facts. Read-only seats are configured by users without a supplied audit workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Daemon-stamped commit hashes, role journals, activity/event logs, raw transport evidence/replay and disclosure checks. - Closure path: Facts can be inspected by an operator or peer; the standard product has no audit verdict/corrective-action protocol for a particular completion claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent fact capture is useful evidence but is not itself discretionary complementary audit; tests, developer reviews and optional advisory seats do not supply an installed audit function. - Evidence: [Scheduler status/journal/check tools][R1], [engine transport evidence][R2], [evidence store][R9], [role design][R6], [line tools][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A user can organize reviewer lines through general messages; that application would need separate function/independence/closure evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider budget changes, new model choices and suggested project patches are available current inputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped intelligence actor develops future-oriented capability options and closes their return into current capability. - Decision owner: Operator chooses reconfiguration/upgrades; ordinary lines may submit untrusted patch proposals. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: proposechange storage, project reload/rebind, provider-budget failover and optional local teacher lifecycle. - Closure path: Proposals are explicitly never applied by Dromon; teacher code only loads/unloads a local model. Rebinding/failover responds to present availability rather than prospective adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A future roadmap, a model called teacher, context handoff or a current capacity switch is not S4. Generic operator patch handling does not create a function-specific parent adaptation loop. - Evidence: [Proposal no-apply contract][R10], [teacher boundary][R11], [role failover][R6], [reload/rebind][R3]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Repository-development/dogfood decisions and future Rust/Keleusma ports are adjacent.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No function-specific identity/ultimate-policy decision-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Access/disclosure rules, operator messages, project instructions and role terms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identity-level issue is specifically adjudicated and returned by ultimate authority as a new governing policy. - Decision owner: Operator configures the project and its permission/disclosure boundaries; agents have explicitly bounded local tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Operator/line address attribution, private socket trust, disclosure checks, tool/recipient policies, protected state and role rules. - Closure path: Configuration and operator answers alter current execution, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue/decision protocol or S3–S4 balance closure is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator is a real parent for S3 current control; that does not promote all configuration or question replies to S5. - Evidence: [Operator and line authority split][R3], [line tools/policy][R4], [project configuration][R7], [scheduler trust and question semantics][R1]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: First-party development documents record human design decisions, but those are not an installed policy actor in an arbitrary user project.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b8dbf77260552d46200e82343dce1fc8cd20a3db/assessments/py/pydromon.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b8dbf77260552d46200e82343dce1fc8cd20a3db\">b8dbf77 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T21:09:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nagisanzenin/INIT-Agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">INIT-Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nagisanzenin/INIT-Agent/tree/1b8b407d2716739a4cfaff6a1751c2f968425edb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1b8b407d2716739a4cfaff6a1751c2f968425edb\">1b8b407 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Convert coding requests into tool-mediated work, revise actions after observed outcomes and return results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing code/task state, model results, tool failures, user steering and interrupted sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select subsequent code/tool actions and responses from the model&#39;s observations. - Decision owner: Mutable agent-body model in the normal supported session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model I/O types, tool dispatcher, context/log, kernel process lifecycle, Root sandbox and egress proxy. - Closure path: Input/context → model-selected tool call → dispatched tool result in the conversation → next model decision. - Boundary reachability: The installed init-agent launches the shipped body loop through the kernel; tool use is not borrowed from the provider&#39;s internal agent service. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party model chooses substantive tool use and adjusts after feedback; Root only constrains execution and supplies transport. - Evidence: seed loop, model tool-call types, tool dispatcher, body boot. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection only; no paid provider or installed-session E2E execution was performed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately evidenced first-party inter-S1 interference attenuation loop at this chosen recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential interference among multiple independent S1 operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific inter-S1 conflict-resolution right established. - Decision owner: None evidenced for S2 in the normal distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded delegation, serial body turn execution, process/sandbox separation and ordinary result return. - Closure path: Delegation and result reporting support S1 task decomposition, without an evidenced S2-specific coordination correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Multiple processes and a delegate tool do not independently demonstrate an interference/oscillation witness and subsequent coordination feedback. - Evidence: delegate implementation, delegate tool, system architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This does not assert that a custom future fleet cannot implement S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Parent-controlled current recovery and runtime constraint changes over the one live agent-and-Root system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe operation, health regressions, continued erroneous work, spend/exposure concerns and failed self-changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect system status and select owner panic/stop/recovery/rollback rather than simply accept the current run. - Decision owner: Legitimate human operator via the Root&#39;s owner channel; Root executes the returned choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Root status snapshot, owner socket/CLI, signed lineage, sandbox, watchdog and snapshot restore. - Closure path: Root current status and warnings → owner chooses panic, stop, resume or rollback → Root changes currently permitted execution/recovered tuple. - Boundary reachability: init-root status, attach, panic, resume and Root recovery are supported installed owner commands, not contributor CI or a suggested external management plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The parent selects consequential whole-system intervention; deterministic Root enforcement and autonomous threshold rollback alone are not credited as agent-owned S3 discretion. - Whole-system current view: Root publishes live slot/mode/level/charter, process state, spend, snapshots and fault/lineage status for the deployed system. - Current-control decision scope: Stop or resume current work and restore/restrict the active body state and access envelope. - Evidence: Root owner CLI, Root contract, README controls. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Parent path is local owner control, not an autonomous multi-agent S3 or organizational governance of repository contributors.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: A Root-sealed, agent-inaccessible regression check of evolved bodies is present; its ownership as the Methodology&#39;s discretionary complementary S3 audit remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Self-reported successful mutation concealing functional regression or benchmark gaming. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Challenge the current body with unseen tasks and return regression/rollback decisions. - Decision owner: Sealed deterministic Root evaluator executes tests and thresholds; no separately evidenced autonomous discretionary audit judge is identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Root-only sealed task store, canaries, paired comparisons, noise floors, signed audit records and rollback mechanism. - Closure path: Sealed task results → deterministic regression verdict → rollback/demotion or accepted audited pointer, affecting subsequent body operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Complementary first-party access and correction exist, but the recorded judge is deterministic enforcement. Further bounded analysis is needed to decide whether the established function&#39;s published ownership is C, — or unresolved under Methodology 0.3.7. - Evidence: Root sealed evaluation contract, Root module wiring, promotion gate contract. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: low for ownership, high for shipped sealed infrastructure. - Caveats: This is not an independent reviewer run or proof of successful model performance.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Propose and deploy changes to the agent&#39;s future operating capabilities from observed task/tool limitations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing requirements, repeated tool failures, inefficiencies and capability mismatch with prospective work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model chooses edits to its own prompt, extension/tool set or compiled body and requests their promotion. - Decision owner: Agent model authors the adaptation option; Root/kernel safety and performance gates may reject it without owning option generation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Editable body/extension worktree, initctl promote, G0–G4 tests, staged process hot-swap, lineage and safety rollback. - Closure path: Observed limitations → agent-authored source/extension change → promotion/gates → accepted live body/tool capability used on later turns; rejected change returns evidence for another attempt. - Boundary reachability: Self-edit is a documented native agent capability; released kernel/Root perform the promotion and can preserve conversation state through a core swap. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The source modification and future option are model-selected; a fixed compiler/test/holdout guard has veto power but does not independently choose the proposed enhancement. - External distinction: Performance and tool demands of tasks/environment relative to present agent capabilities. - Future / prospective distinction: Revised future tool/core behavior, not merely saving a current conversation. - Adaptation option generated: New/modified skills, tools and Rust body code. - Path back into current capability / S3: Accepted promotion changes the running body; Root can roll it back based on current outcome. - Evidence: README self-evolution workflow, agent evolution contract, kernel gate contract, extension authoring. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Shipped self-edit closure is not a claim of measured net capability gain; the README explicitly notes that persistent benefit is not yet demonstrated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Keep ultimate authority over the agent&#39;s identity and non-editable safety/extension charter outside the self-modifying actor. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent efforts to redefine its own authority, protected access and evolution permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select and explicitly apply the Root charter defining the owner&#39;s non-negotiable authority envelope. - Decision owner: Human owner; the independent Root validates, signs and enforces the choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Owner-only charter.toml, charter checks/digest, active charter state, Root sandbox/permission rules and separation from the agent zone. - Closure path: Owner changes charter and invokes charter check --apply → Root validates/activates it → the next runtime decisions are constrained by the new authoritative charter. - Boundary reachability: The installed Root CLI exposes owner-only charter check/apply while the mutable agent is sandboxed away from Root home. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The self-modifying agent does not control the protected charter or Root. Runtime permission requests alone were not counted; the evidenced claim is the explicit higher-level charter authority and return to operation. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Whether and under which hard non-editable constraints the agent may alter itself, gain access or survive a regression. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Human owner only; Root records/enforces the decision. - Return-to-operation path: Active charter affects subsequent agent-zone sandbox and promotion operations. - Evidence: Root charter implementation, owner apply CLI, Root architecture. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Does not infer project-level S5 governance from the existence of maintainers, contributors or release approvals.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/061d70e295c9fa9b3138e08a1b5767c5344709f1/assessments/in/init-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 061d70e295c9fa9b3138e08a1b5767c5344709f1\">061d70e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:56:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/S0ra233/Sora-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sora Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/S0ra233/Sora-Harness/tree/cbfddafca44ff18f80181943ce17ba1d271ed7a6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cbfddafca44ff18f80181943ce17ba1d271ed7a6\">cbfddaf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce local tool-mediated task results, translations and project artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: input formats, provider/tool errors, partial results, project state and user steering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose operations/tools and revise task work after observed results. - Decision owner: the model-backed main actor and supported child actors in their respective task scopes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentLoop, provider backends, tool registry, workspace restrictions, step/call limits, checkpoints and subprocess execution. - Closure path: tool results append to model history; errors or partial state lead to revised actions, while terminal results are persisted. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actors leaves wrappers/storage/guards without discretionary problem solving. - Evidence: desktop/runtime/agent/loop.py; desktop/application/conversations.py; desktop/runtime/subagents/runner.py; desktop/tools/registry.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Bounded source assessment of the supported distribution; no live model or performance claim. - Boundary reachability: ConversationApplication invokes the first-party AgentLoop; the child runner constructs that same application and sends/resumes a message.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent logical and physical write interference between concurrent parent/child operational tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workers sharing a project can overwrite the same target or publish partial/inconsistent translation packages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose disjoint work/target-ID ranges and separate result paths, revise overlapping scopes and decide serialized final publication. - Decision owner: the main model actor following the supplied subagent/tool and collaboration mode; locks only enforce exclusive writes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoped subagent tools, actual child status/results, send/continue messages, shared mutation-lock propagation and file-lock serialization. - Closure path: the main actor reads actual results, checks missing/duplicate/out-of-range IDs and file contents, corrects directly or sends revised child instructions, then commits the reconciled package serially. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the main actor a lock can serialize two incompatible edits but cannot choose semantically disjoint task boundaries or reconcile their outputs. - Evidence: desktop/plugins/subagent/tools.py; desktop/application/subagents.py; desktop/runtime/subagents/runner.py; desktop/application/mutationgate.py; desktop/tools/registry.py; desktop/plugincatalog.py; desktop/application/skills.py; skills/game-translation/references/collaboration.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Optional enabled multi-actor mode, not every single-agent chat. Configured child models/profiles are normal deployment inputs. The lock alone would not justify A. - Distinct S1 units: main task actor and independent-history child translation, review or research task actors with separate outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: shared-workspace writes, conflicting edits to the same target and overlapping or incomplete target-ID coverage. - Attenuating coordination relation: agent-selected non-overlapping ranges/result files, central serial apply/build and corrective scope messages, backed by cross-process write exclusion. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: real child states and artifact coverage checks cause corrected delegation/continuation or revised publication decisions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: first-party tool instructions and collaboration rules explicitly identify shared-file/logical collisions and complete-ID publication, rather than merely exposing a generic mailbox. - Boundary reachability: enabled subagent profiles bind real tool capabilities in SubagentManager; the built-in tool description supplies coordination rules to the model. The translation plugin&#39;s skill root and SkillService make the concrete disjoint-ID/serial-commit workflow available in the supported product mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No supplied whole-application discretionary current-control function is established beyond bounded task coordination. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child runtime limits, failed work and local task progression. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent status/cancel/continue, per-role step/time/call limits, run records and main-agent task aggregation. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: desktop/application/subagentprofiles.py; desktop/application/subagents.py; desktop/plugins/subagent/tools.py; desktop/runtime/runs.py; skills/game-translation/references/collaboration.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No material independent complementary audit path is supplied beyond ordinary operational review. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete child claims and translation/schema errors are checked in the production workflow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: actual-file reads, acceptance reports, review subtasks, tool receipts and run/checkpoint logs. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: skills/game-translation/references/collaboration.md; skills/game-translation/references/candidate-review.md; skills/game-translation/SKILL.md; desktop/plugins/subagent/tools.py; desktop/runtime/session.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Independent histories and raw-file availability alone do not turn every review into S3. This is bounded to the supplied product/skill modes, not a prohibition on a custom audit design.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environment-to-capability adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported game layouts, context limits and task-relevant reference information. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plugin/skill loading, retrieval, compaction and project-local adapter authoring. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: skills/game-translation/references/custom-adapters.md; skills/game-translation/SKILL.md; desktop/application/skills.py; desktop/runtime/context/compaction.py; desktop/plugincatalog.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication-return path is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary user task scope, prompt presets, permissions and plugin enablement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompt configuration, user questions/steering, per-profile instructions and tool allowlists. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: desktop/prompts/config.py; desktop/prompts/assembly.py; desktop/application/subagentprofiles.py; desktop/tools/contracts.py; skills/game-translation/SKILL.md; README.en.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/de3f3196a0c3cd85133074d47477e2fddede7f68/assessments/so/sora-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit de3f3196a0c3cd85133074d47477e2fddede7f68\">de3f319 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T19:04:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ashishpatel26/Enterprise-Agent-Harness-Project-Template\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Enterprise Agent Harness Template</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ashishpatel26/Enterprise-Agent-Harness-Project-Template/tree/02d40b3cd712af07d5e5ccab12edee9980b988fe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 02d40b3cd712af07d5e5ccab12edee9980b988fe\">02d40b3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Distinct research, coding and review specialists perform model-selected work for their assigned subtasks; supervisor itself uses a model-directed delegation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Research/document changes, coding outcomes, partial/incorrect drafts, tool responses, task-specific failures and approval delays. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Each specialist model chooses its action or answer from a fresh subtask prompt and any available permitted tools; the supervisor model chooses delegate tool calls and final composition. - Decision owner: Configured specialist agent model at each child-run S1 boundary, implemented via the runnable AgentLoop/tool pipeline. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-agent YAML model/tool/limits, isolated child contexts, standard tool execution/guards, checkpoints, retries/recovery and explicit RunState outcomes. - Closure path: Parent task → model-chosen delegate call → distinct child model/tool/observation loop → child result/error → parent feedback/next decision. - Boundary reachability: First-party Harness.build installs delegation and validates shipped supervisor YAML&#39;s specialist names; delegate calls createrun/execute, not merely an example external orchestrator. Integration tests exercise parallel independent children with fake LLMs; configured production inference endpoints were not exercised here. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Child models own discretionary task steps; the host executes checks and records state. The supervisor&#39;s choice of subtask does not erase independent specialist S1 agency. - Evidence: loop, runtime wiring, child registry, integration fixture. - Basis: Installed separate autonomous child-run model/tool feedback, with source and test-case corroboration. - Confidence: High for source-distribution reachability; runtime paid-model outcome not independently verified. - Caveats: Child availability and model quality depend on operator configuration; test assertions with FakeLLM prove paths rather than live autonomy quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: A specific installed attenuating channel for inter-S1 operational conflict is not demonstrated, despite real sub-agent delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overlapping specialist tasks, incompatible answers or resource contention could arise, but the source does not encode detection/settlement of reciprocal S1 clashes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Supervisor can assign and repeat work, and static registry validates allowed children; no evidence of separate horizontal S1-to-S1 disturbance arbitration or an S2-specific feedback relation. - Decision owner: Supervisor model as current task manager; deterministic roster/permission validation handles routing, not inter-unit conflict mediation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagents allowlist, parallel delegate calls, child IDs and shared tenant/tools with separate contexts. - Closure path: Parent delegation → child replies → supervisor current control; no distinct conflict signal between S1 children → attenuating settlement → reciprocal changed operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Delegation, tool routing, serial/parallel sequencing and shared budget accounting alone do not establish the VSM S2 function. - Evidence: delegation, supervisor, integration tests. - Basis: Bounded absence review against the strongest multi-agent candidate. - Confidence: Medium-high; positive S3 supervision is not automatically positive S2 conflict attenuation. - Caveats: A future operator policy could require or build explicit inter-specialist conflict arbitration, but no independent pathway is currently closed in the shipped model.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: A live supervisor controls the present specialist-work portfolio for one parent task by selecting subagents, reviewing outcomes and adjusting assignments under a shared task budget. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Diverse subtasks, inadequate specialist output, failed/paused child runs, concurrent execution and aggregate token/cost limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-driven supervisor chooses which allowed specialist gets each subtask (possibly in parallel), sees child answers/errors/pause status, can delegate corrective work, decides when to synthesize the overall answer. - Decision owner: Configured supervisor model; installed delegate tool and harness enforce its chosen child-run requests and report results. The static budget cap is not itself the S3 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: supervisor.yaml and supervisor@v1 instructions, child roster, depth ceiling, deterministic child IDs for crash recovery, rolled-up child usage/cost, isolated subtask execution and returned status/answer observations. - Closure path: Supervisor task view → delegate choices → child S1 task execution → successful/error/awaiting-approval tool outcome → supervisor&#39;s next model turn and re-delegation/answer choice, with aggregate cost/usage enforced by parent loop. - Whole-system current view: At the declared parent-task recursion, the supervisor sees its approved specialist roster and actual result/error messages from each dispatched child; shared parent budget counts child usage. It does not see all users/tenants or manage an enterprise fleet. - Current-control decision scope: Current assignment, follow-up/reassignment, review sequencing and acceptance/synthesis of the research/coding/review portfolio, not long-term system strategy or ultimate-policy rights. - Boundary reachability: The first-party composition root installs the delegate registry, shipped supervisor config enumerates specialists, prompt explicitly directs re-delegation on bad results, and integration test runs independent children concurrently and returns results/cost to the parent. This is operational first-party S3 for the bounded task organization, not the mere class name Supervisor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model can choose or revise present portfolio assignments after actual child feedback. Deterministic guard/leases/idempotency alone would not qualify; positive mapping depends on the installed decision path plus child autonomy. - Evidence: supervisor instructions, shipped roster, delegate return, tests. - Basis: Evidence-backed installed supervisory current-control closed loop across autonomous child work, sharply scoped to one task organization. - Confidence: Medium-high (decision-path closure proven at source; live provider correctness and conflict handling not tested). - Caveats: A supervisor may simply accept poor outputs; normative quality of its choices is untested and S3 is not S3 independent audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary reality-checking auditor verifies task claims via alternate evidence and closes findings into current management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Bad research claims or coding changes can be questioned by review specialist or offline eval regression tests, but their installed evidence paths do not make an independent live audit circuit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: review agent has no first-party truth-access tools and receives only a supervisor-authored subtask prompt; audit log hashes observed events without verifying their truth. Eval runner is a separate test/CI workflow, not a returned S3 corrective channel for live work. - Decision owner: Supervisor decides whether to request review and whether to act on it; review model lacks independent access to workspace/reality evidence, offline scorer/CI operator lives outside the task controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Optional review sub-agent, tamper-evident AuditLog.verify, policy safety guard, offline test expectations and regression baseline comparison. - Closure path: Supervisor-provided material → review critique → supervisor continuation (an ordinary S1 report); or offline test → regression report. Neither creates independent complementary evidence → audit verdict → enforceable S3 correction through an accountable audit role. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Separate model identity alone does not imply independent observational access; hash-chain integrity only proves log continuity, and a CI pass is not an independent audit of actual deployment claims. - Evidence: review spec, audit log, evals, delegation. - Basis: Explicit separation of reviewer model, audit-log transport and offline eval against required S3 complementary-independence conditions. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An operator could independently audit via external tools/records; the current review agent may use its learned knowledge but has no distinct first-party reality observation path in its YAML.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No distinct future-environment scanning/adaptation authority governs the whole task organization beyond current user work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New provider availability, changing task types, tool/schema policy, evolving skill capability and future usage forecasts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human operator controls agent YAML/provider/plugin updates; specialist research serves current delegated question rather than strategic capability change. - Decision owner: Developer/tenant for future architecture; supervisor for present delegation only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plugin extension points, config reload/deployment, memory stores, eval baselines and model/provider retries. - Closure path: Tasks and current tool feedback → user answer or manual version update; no autonomous prospective environmental intelligence → option selection → S3 negotiation/adopted future capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A research sub-agent and memory embeddings may help present tasks, but do not install an organizational future-modeling/strategy actor. - Evidence: architecture, research spec, runtime. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Tool-using agents can discuss the future within individual tasks; that is not a shipped S4 organizational feedback loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate identity/policy deliberation or legitimate parent-governed identity-level mandate returns to the supervisor-led organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Security permissions, sensitive data, tool use, tenant accountability and human approval requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Policy YAML and operator settings define guards; human reviewer approves/denies individual risky tool calls and may be prohibited from approving their own call. None evidences identity-level policy revision. - Decision owner: External operator/security policy author for static mandates and approval reviewer for individual actions, not a packaged S5 ultimate governance role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SafetyGuard, default policy, ApprovalService, four-eyes switch, audit log, data redaction and tenant API authentication. - Closure path: Policy rule → tool pause → human yes/no → resumed or denied action. This is a closed ordinary permission loop, not identity/mission issue → ultimate authority → revised governance mandate to the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Rigorous approvals and tamper-evident security logging enforce chosen policy, but do not confer first-party S5 or the Methodology&#39;s parent-mode P without a function-specific identity decision right. - Evidence: approvals, guard, policy. - Basis: Bounded absence review distinguishing action authorization from organizational identity-policy closure. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: In a real enterprise, external human governance likely exists; it is not an encoded operational S5 mode within this shipped template.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c962f1ebb771b64d589d68a59cb87abe2ab44f96/assessments/en/enterprise-agent-harness-template.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c962f1ebb771b64d589d68a59cb87abe2ab44f96\">c962f1e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T18:16:51+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhanghaojie513/Dougong\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dougong</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhanghaojie513/Dougong/tree/5d7ff991baa0da919c580f418ed5415b50ba3a94\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5d7ff991baa0da919c580f418ed5415b50ba3a94\">5d7ff99 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed problem solving through iterative use of file, search, shell, MCP and delegated task tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown workspace state, model responses, tool errors, user task changes and subprocess results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose tools and arguments, delegate self-contained subtasks, interpret results and select the next task action or final response. - Decision owner: Primary and child model-backed agent loops; the host supplies protocols but not the concrete solution steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Kernel registration, tool dispatcher, approval policy, JSONL session commit, schema validation, workspace path guard, model connectors, retry and tool-round limits. - Closure path: Prompt/history → IChatClient completion → model-selected tool call → dispatcher execution → tool/child result → subsequent IChatClient turn and final task output. - Boundary reachability: Default SessionComposition registers tools and creates LoopAgentRuntime in both CLI and ACP paths; SubagentTool uses the same runtime class with fresh child history. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing model choice leaves permission enforcement and protocol handling, but no substantive next-step/task-selection discretion. - Evidence: LoopAgentRuntime.RunAsync, SessionComposition tool registration, ToolDispatcher. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for wired model/tool feedback; external provider outcome not executed during this review. - Caveats: Offline probes and advertised provider benchmarks are not used as evidence of real-task completion rates.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate contention between concurrent task-owning sessions or worker calls accessing the same workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overlapping shell/tool mutation and inconsistent shared-workspace state when independently executing S1 cells interfere; specifically two supported ACP sessions entering exclusive workspace actions. - Distinct S1 units: Two concurrently model-operated ACP sessions in one process, and primary/child independent task loops when their scoped tools overlap. - Inter-S1 disturbance: First-party G9 case explicitly models two session gates entering the same workspace and risking simultaneous exclusive operations; unguarded writes can overlap. - Attenuating coordination relation: Process-shared normalized workspace mutex for serial/exclusive tools; a per-dispatcher resource-key read/write gate for declared tool resources; execution lease delays conflicting calls. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The blocked call executes only after the lease clears, then its concrete tool result reaches its requesting agent&#39;s next model turn. The coordination changes the timing/observable world state of subsequent operations; refusal/error is also returned through dispatcher. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The gate explicitly targets a concrete conflict among autonomous work cells (same workspace competing tool mutations), with conflict-dependent exclusion and resumed tool feedback; it is not merely a queue of tasks or parent-to-child dispatch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Apply or revise the actual inter-session resource-exclusion relation. The supplied resource gate deterministically applies declared plans; no autonomous agent is shipped as an owner of shared conflict-policy discretion. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic constructor/runtime resource gate; developers declare tool concurrency and can compose policy authority. Agent models choose tasks but do not decide lock admissibility. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SharedWorkspaceLocks, semaphores, stable resource keys, registry limits and ToolExecutionPlan. - Closure path: Competing tool intents → gate classification and lease acquisition → serialized protected execution → result back to the relevant agent; construction of an adaptive autonomous coordination owner is not part of this standard distribution. - Boundary reachability: CLI/ACP SessionComposition constructs a non-isolated ToolResourceGate; the static shared lock table is reached by serial/workspace-exclusive tool invocations, corroborated by pinned G9 probe. Resource-key locks are instance-scoped and are not claimed to protect arbitrary file edits across independent dispatchers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing agents&#39; discretionary policy leaves the same lock decision and resource exclusion; the function-specific path is real, but not an agent-owned S2 closure, hence C rather than A. - Evidence: ToolResourceGate, G9 cross-session counterproof, SubagentTool resource classification, host wiring. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded coordination; not a claim of universal cross-worker file conflict protection. - Caveats: Asynchronously spawned children outlive the spawn-tool lease; cross-process locks and all resource-key paths are not established.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Allocate and revise current task commitments across the primary agent&#39;s active child-agent work portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel worker availability, failed delegated results, conflicting subtask priorities and finite execution capacity during one task composition. - Whole-system current view: Agent-accessible subagentlist exposes all handles in this composition with current running state, and subagentresult returns terminal status, text, error and consumed tool rounds; getgoal and joblist expose current local task/goal state. - Current-control decision scope: Primary model chooses which child work to launch or postpone, which results to retrieve, whether to execute remaining work itself and what follow-up action to assign, within a four-concurrent-child and bounded-depth envelope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Commit or revise currently active work distribution based on live child and goal results. - Decision owner: Primary conversational model; SubagentRegistry and goal/dispatcher implement its choices but do not choose which task to prioritize. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child handles, concurrency gate, depth bounds, one-shot result collection, goal state, optional job status and tool permission checks. - Closure path: Primary chooses subtask → asynchronous child run → model requests list/result → receives current status/diagnostic → decides to delegate another task, wait, adjust, work directly or finish in a subsequent loop iteration. - Boundary reachability: SessionComposition registers subagent, subagentlist, subagentresult and goal tools with the standard CLI/ACP model dispatcher; the supplied child loop uses the same IChatClient and shared registry. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove primary model discretion and the runtime still enforces slots and reports status but does not decide task allocation, current commitments or recovery. - Evidence: SubagentTools, SubagentRegistry, SessionComposition registration and child runtime. - Basis: structural; agent discretion in the standard tool-call loop is explicit. - Confidence: medium for current-control scope, high for callable feedback path. - Caveats: S3 scope is the one primary-session task portfolio, not a global dashboard controlling all separately instantiated hosts. No parent-governed S3 mode is evidenced by the optional plan approver.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently independent complementary direct-reality audit that reports corrective findings to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool failure, approval denial and stale session reports are ordinarily visible, but no separate auditor authority samples them independently. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently challenge the operational claim with materially different access and return corrective findings to S3; not wired. - Decision owner: Ordinary dispatcher and logger record their own actions; a human plan approver can accept a proposed plan, not audit completed operational claims. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: JSONL commit barrier, ToolDispatcher approval logs, user plan review, diagnostics, self-check comparison suite and subagent result summaries. - Closure path: Tool status returns directly to requesting model or is stored for replay; no independent auditor sampling → judgment → corrective returned control loop is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A child can hypothetically be tasked to review a patch, but the default host has no protected independent reviewer role, separate evidence path or mandatory corrective audit closure. - Evidence: JsonlAgentStore, ToolDispatcher, PlanMode, SubagentTools. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded absence. - Caveats: External code reviewers and offline self-check probes are outside the assessed operational boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped, operationally closed external-prospective capability adaptation process. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model/provider changes, evolving user preferences and plugin capability opportunities could merit adaptation, but the default loop addresses current tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Evaluate future-relevant environmental options and authorize capability change returned to current S3/operations; not supplied as a complete first-party chain. - Decision owner: User/developer supplies tool plugins, provider and preferences; models choose available current task actions and can write plans. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plugin lifecycle/hot swap, skill discovery, optional L1 MemoryStore/MemoryInjector, session compaction and manual config. - Closure path: Whitelisted Enabled memory would be injected into later turns, but the shipped CLI has no writer invoking MemoryStore.PutAsync/AdvanceCursorAsync to create, judge, approve and enable new L1 artifacts from outside/future evidence. No prospective option judgment and capability change loop closes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Temporal persistence and dynamic plugin loading do not make a model-owned strategic adaptation decision; optional memory readback is a support surface only. - Evidence: MemoryArtifact, MemoryInjector, SessionComposition, README.en L1 claims. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high at the supported runtime boundary. - Caveats: The separate experiment proving preference adherence does not establish a deployed preference-learning decision owner; embedded SDK consumers could add the missing cycle independently.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime resolution of organizational identity or ultimate-policy dilemmas with authoritative return into subsequent work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dangerous tools, security/privacy switches, permission escalation and plan approval are execution risks within a preselected organizational purpose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide a contested identity-level/ultimate-policy matter and enact the new constitutional constraint in the operating harness; no such first-party path. - Decision owner: User/operator configures permission/environment, human approver authorizes individual tool or plan transitions, and developer chooses fixed policy implementation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SecuritySwitches, PermissionPolicyApprover, approval content binding, restricted L1 preference whitelist and plan review. - Closure path: User-approved actions change current task execution; configuration fixed at startup bounds tool access. No identity/policy proposal → legitimate ultimate authority → governing response → durable changed operation loop exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Approval is local task authorization; neither model nor operator tool prompt resolves identity or ultimate policy of the operating organization. - Evidence: PermissionPolicyApprover, PlanMode, SecuritySwitches via host, MemoryArtifact whitelist. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded absence. - Caveats: Maintainer repository-level governance is outside the deployed runtime organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1ab3c938cffea0f2611e346bfce4afc731b23138/assessments/do/dougong.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1ab3c938cffea0f2611e346bfce4afc731b23138\">1ab3c93 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T18:53:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amiya-special/Dynaflow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DynaFlow</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amiya-special/Dynaflow/tree/7451639d197ef844b4157cf40eadac68e9eba8ca\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7451639d197ef844b4157cf40eadac68e9eba8ca\">7451639 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: a model-driven subagent interprets a delegated question subtask, chooses its own supported research/tool calls and produces a substantive answer or structured result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain source material, changing factual evidence, calculations, research/tool response differences and errors in executing an assigned subproblem. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose how to query configured tools, reason about their actual outputs, retry task-local reasoning and deliver a substantive result to the workflow. - Decision owner: the DynaFlow-managed subagent model invoked through Session.worker and the agent() hook; the independent model API service supplies computation but its unrelated internal policies are not borrowed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflowworker.system.txt role constraints, model-compatible Chat Completions adapter, allowed tool schemas, Python executetool bridge, Docker credential isolation, output schema validation, journal and progress events. - Closure path: main workflow delegates subtask → configured worker model interprets prompt and requests relevant tools → tool/model results return into its message history → final text/StructuredOutput is delivered or explicitly marked dead. - Boundary reachability: src/dynaflow/workflow.py provides a real worker model-call loop and Python host tool execution, while workflowruntime/src/engine/hooks.ts runs the worker and returns validated results to the JavaScript script in supported dynamic/forced modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the host cannot perform task-specific research/calculate the final substantive result by parsing the static workflow alone; the model-driven worker makes nontrivial task decisions. - Evidence: pinned src/dynaflow/workflow.py, src/dynaflow/systemprompts.py, workflowruntime/src/engine/hooks.ts, README mode table. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for reachable owned subagent execution, not for actual answer accuracy on unrun providers. - Caveats: model serving and externally configured search APIs are separate providers; a default direct baseline is not the basis for counting multi-agent role ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct lateral inter-S1 disturbance attenuation and return path beyond workflow scheduling/resource constraints is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent requests contend for a configured maximum number of execution slots and tokens, but no independent cross-worker semantic collision or negotiated working relationship is specified in the shipped path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the semaphore grants/reclaims permits, the engine numbers calls, and script dependencies order tasks; these are constructor-authored scheduling controls, not a supported S2 conflict-resolution right over peer S1 relations. - Decision owner: static engine limits and caller-authored workflow script, not an identifiable lateral coordination agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Semaphore.acquire/release, global agent cap, shared budget, Promise.all-style workflow orchestration, journal sequence and cancellation. - Closure path: one call awaits capacity and later proceeds when a permit is released; this closes a bounded scheduler resource constraint without showing disturbance-specific feedback changing separate S1 units&#39; conflicting operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel execution and a concurrency cap do not automatically establish the S2 function; no source-evidenced first-party peer contention arbitration/reconciliation was identified. - Distinct S1 units: the individually prompted subagents called by the generated workflow. - Inter-S1 disturbance: potential competition for capacity or contradictory research outputs, but a distinct implemented peer conflict path is not shown. - Attenuating coordination relation: configured semaphore and script dependency sequencing; neither is evidence of an S2-specific cross-worker conflict feedback loop. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a waiting slot delays launch, but no path-specific conflict ruling is carried to an already operating peer to regulate its subsequent activity. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: no S2-specific evidence survives this distinction; hence absence rather than an inflated S2=C score. - Evidence: pinned workflowruntime/src/engine/concurrency.ts, hooks.ts, runWorkflow.ts, src/dynaflow/workflow.py. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high at the frozen shipped boundary. - Caveats: a user-generated Workflow could voluntarily implement specialized peer coordination, but arbitrary user-authored scripts are not a guaranteed first-party function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: the main model dynamically plans, launches, monitors, assesses and modifies the current subagent Workflow to complete the operator&#39;s question. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed subagents, incomplete/invalid delivery, insufficient evidence, changing research direction, stalled/aborted work and uncertain selection between direct and delegated execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: generate task-specific workflow scripts; choose serial/parallel subagent composition and verification stages; call Workflow/TaskOutput/TaskStop; revise or rerun workflow when receipts indicate incomplete or contradicted evidence; decide whether to synthesize the final answer. - Decision owner: first-party-instructed main model in Session.run, in the supported dynamic/forced runtime; deterministic engine/host rules act as subordinate gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: main role prompt, mode/forced policy, tool schemas and asynchronous Session.launch/output, status receipts, journal resume, bounded retries, deadline/cancel paths and host&#39;s valid-cloud-agent finalization predicate. - Closure path: user question and current execution constraints → main model chooses/generates a Workflow → subagents work and send progress/results → TaskOutput/delivery/status returned → main model revises/reruns/stops or completes answer under immutable host acceptance rules. - Boundary reachability: src/dynaflow/assets/workflowmain.system.txt and forcedprompt.txt require the described lead action; src/dynaflow/workflow.py exposes the actual tool calls, stores and returns receipts, and supports re-launch/resume and output blocking. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: unlike the fixed semaphore or outer question queue, the model chooses and changes the task-specific subagent program and how to respond to unsatisfactory execution, giving it a bona fide current whole-workflow management right. - Whole-system current view: active local Workflow invocations, completed run IDs/receipts, failed or valid delivery, current subagent responses and original question acceptance requirements. - Current-control decision scope: question-local orchestration/recovery/answer readiness; not global resource governance over multiple independently funded projects or stable policy change. - Evidence: pinned src/dynaflow/workflow.py (Session.run, launch, output), first-party main/forced prompts, workflowruntime/src/engine/runWorkflow.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the supported main-agent-in-the-loop operating mode. - Caveats: actual use of the optional dynamic Workflow is conditional; in direct, no subagent organization exists. The model decides current workflow design, but cannot bypass host tool restriction, budget, delivery validity or user stop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no shipped separately tasked, independently evidenced challenge of an operating agent&#39;s correctness claim with a mandated corrective feedback return is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: malformed JSON, empty/invalid deliveries and model outputs lacking required successful-agent provenance are caught, but these are validity/reachability guards, not independent evidence-based challenge of substantive claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: constructor code checks schemas, transport receipts and whether a successful configured subagent delivered results; no distinct substantive reviewer actor is given a protected right to inspect contradicting primary evidence and return adjudicated findings. - Decision owner: deterministic validateAgentDelivery and host finalization predicates for format/provenance; model-driven main agent may inspect results but is also author of the plan and final synthesis, not an independent assessor by default. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: StructuredOutput JSON Schema, validateDelivery, validcloudagents, TaskOutput, run journal and optional user-created script verification steps. - Closure path: subagent output → schema/empty-result and successful-delivery checks → accepted/rejected receipt or current orchestration re-run; no default complementary channel challenges whether the answer is true against independently obtained source/acceptance evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: syntactic validation is constructor logic, and ad hoc optional model-generated verification does not establish a separate first-party audit mandate or epistemic independence. - Claim being audited: supposed successful and correct response to the original research question. - Ordinary reporting path: subagent task output/status delivered through the host. - Complementary access path: no standard required reviewer with its own independent primary-source acquisition; the main model can read task outputs and optionally design a verification stage. - Independence boundary: no deployed distinct reviewer identity, read-only isolation or mandatory independently sourced cross-check shown. - Who acts on findings: the main agent may rerun after failed delivery, but that is ordinary current-control recovery without an independently warranted challenge verdict. - Evidence: pinned workflowruntime/src/engine/deliveryValidation.ts, runWorkflow.ts, src/dynaflow/workflow.py, src/dynaflow/assets/workflowmain.system.txt. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a specific generated script could use separate verification subagents and alter a future task-specific score, but existence of arbitrary script capability is not evidence of a shipped S3 audit loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no ongoing first-party environment intelligence function that anticipates changes and modifies the longer-term capabilities/strategy of the DynaFlow work organization is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel user questions and alternate model research sources are present-work inputs, not demonstrated changes in future capability demand. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses an appropriate Workflow for a single question, and developers may change runtime code, but no bounded S4 agent authorizes prospective operating capability adaptations. - Decision owner: question-local main model for current-work plan and external maintainers/users for updates or future workloads. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dynamically generated workflow scripts, run records, mode configuration and academic research documentation. - Closure path: incoming question → generated current-work script → result; no current environmental trend → prospective adaptation proposal → installed new future organizational capability loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: dynamic per-task program generation is not automatically S4; the generated script executes and is discarded/archived as that task&#39;s procedure, rather than constituting a persistent policy/capability shift. - Evidence: pinned README, src/dynaflow/workflow.py, src/dynaflow/runner.py, workflowruntime/src/engine/runWorkflow.ts. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human research team may analyze DynaFlow experiments to change later algorithms, outside this assessed runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no internally closed legitimate ultimate identity/policy decision function for a DynaFlow installation is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: API credentials, allowed research tools, direct-versus-workflow policy, Docker capability restrictions, quotas and required delivery are fixed by constructor configuration/user mode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: mode selection and engine constraints come from administrator/configuration; main model cannot rewrite the execution rules or determine ultimate purpose outside the supplied question. - Decision owner: external deployer/evaluation owner and static constructor policy rather than an autonomous organization-level constitutional actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mode prompt, configured tool list, credential isolation, Docker network/privilege restrictions, budget/agent caps and STOPBYUSER marker. - Closure path: externally specified policy → enforced model/tool permissions and delivery constraints; no legitimate identity/ultimate-policy dispute is adjudicated and returned to govern the future organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: obeying a forced mode and enforcing security restrictions are real constraints, but not first-party S5 political/identity decisions. - Evidence: pinned src/dynaflow/configuration.py, src/dynaflow/workflow.py, src/dynaflow/runner.py, src/dynaflow/assets/forcedprompt.txt. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: high for the narrow supplied runtime. - Caveats: the paper authors and external academic laboratory have policy and strategy outside this executable product; these are not imported as its first-party S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/83a9181e16af7074e956c39286049a23c0cad297/assessments/dy/dynaflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 83a9181e16af7074e956c39286049a23c0cad297\">83a9181 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:33:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ruslantrachuk/dark-factory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dark factory</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ruslantrachuk/dark-factory/tree/a56142f7e79efdfa9aa8acee7596974b4efea174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a56142f7e79efdfa9aa8acee7596974b4efea174\">a56142f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: deliver project changes and bounded correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: underspecified implementation details, code/test failures and returned findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose implementation, tests and bounded fixes within the approved request. - Decision owner: first-party builder role instantiated by the installed delivery workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: external host/model, source-defined role and skills, scoped worktree/tools, schemas, tests, commit and task-state scripts. - Closure path: owned builder prompt → edits/tests/result → integration and review → fix agent or explicit incomplete result → committed branch. - Boundary reachability: feature command and feature-pipeline manifest invoke shipped named workflows and builder role; no private author runtime is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: workflow scaffolding alone cannot implement arbitrary requirements; the configured builder exercises local coding discretion. - Evidence: plugins/dark-factory/workflows/feature-auto.js; plugins/dark-factory/workflows/feature-core.js; plugins/dark-factory/agents/builder.md; plugins/dark-factory/templates/feature-pipeline/MANIFEST.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Requires the documented host/toolchain and project setup; seatbelt-only mode has no such complete delivery loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent builder edits and conflicts with unmerged work. - Distinct S1 units: separate feature/subtask builder cells in parallel-wave mode. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two cells write the same planned code file or concurrently commit the shared working tree. - Attenuating coordination relation: pairwiseDisjoint forces overlapping waves to run sequentially; disjoint workers do not commit, and a single integrator commits the wave. feature-auto also returns overlaps in-flight branch before build. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: overlap changes execution from parallel to serial, or stops a feature until the branch is merged/restarted; each later wave observes integrated predecessor state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the dispatch decision explicitly tests the actual shared-file collision surface and constrains subsequent writes/commits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concrete cross-builder file/commit interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the prescribed safe wave/stop response to intersecting file scopes. - Decision owner: developer-authored collision rule and configured override; the overlap agent gathers intersections rather than owning discretionary organizational settlement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task graph, file sets, sequential/parallel executor, worktree and integrator role. - Closure path: planned file intersection → serialize/stop → constrained later builder operation. - Boundary reachability: the installed feature-core pipeline mode intentionally exposes this function-specific path; session mode is a single writer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the fixed rule is not autonomous coordination discretion; a function-specific path exists, but autonomous coordination authority must still be composed. - Evidence: plugins/dark-factory/workflows/feature-core.js; plugins/dark-factory/workflows/feature-auto.js; plugins/dark-factory/workflows/README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: C is limited to evidenced file/commit interference, not semantic coordination or generic task sequencing.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate the project&#39;s current outstanding commitments on behalf of the whole. - Whole-system current view: triage facts/report account for open and in-progress tasks, stale work, main-branch completion evidence, queue limit and fresh tasks excluded from detailed review. - Current-control decision scope: retire obsolete/completed/unsupported commitments or defer work; retain exceptions selected by the project owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unbounded accumulated task commitments and stale work competing with present delivery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which proposed close/defer actions become authoritative. - Decision owner: legitimate local project owner answering the Remote Control queue-review session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: timer/fleet, read-only product-owner model proposal, numbered report/TTL, choose parser and bd CLI. - Closure path: project queue facts → proposal/report to owner → yes/except/number selection → triage.py apply executes only selected close/defer decisions → later queue-driven work changes. - Boundary reachability: fleet provisions the first-party triage path for a project with bd; the same shipped script opens the owner-visible session and implements apply. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model recommends; no commitment is closed without the owner&#39;s returned answer. Static agent/credit caps are enforcement, not an additional A S3 mode. - Evidence: plugins/dark-factory/scripts/triage.py; plugins/dark-factory/scripts/fleet.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Only the configured local project queue is covered; no claim of autonomous whole-fleet management or cryptographic human authentication.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge builder claims of a correct, appropriately tested implementation through complementary source inspection. - Claim being audited: the builder&#39;s completed/tested branch respects requirements, project invariants and test-layer contracts. - Ordinary reporting path: builder result and green scoped/full gates. - Complementary access path: fresh reviewer reads the actual branch diff, neighboring source and standards through test/invariant/simplify lenses. - Independence boundary: separately instantiated read-only reviewer role, separate context, no authority to alter its target or decide merge; it can report defects despite builder/gate success. - Who acts on findings: a separate fix builder verifies/rejects scoped findings with reasons, edits and reruns gates; unresolved critical findings prevent a clean result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: self-reporting blind spots, omitted tests, invariant breaches and false clean claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge evidence-backed findings against raw source and the stated contract. - Decision owner: fresh first-party reviewer agent; fix/triage authority is separately owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role tools/prompt, finding schemas, severity filtering and workflow outcome gate. - Closure path: raw diff inspection → structured findings → bounded fix or justified rejection → tests/commit and blockersRemaining/error result. - Boundary reachability: feature-core invokes shipped reviewer role after build, and passes findings into its shipped Fix stage; the manifest packages this into the supported feature command. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the independent audit judgment is model-owned; schema parsing and gates only transport/enforce it. - Evidence: plugins/dark-factory/workflows/feature-core.js; plugins/dark-factory/agents/reviewer.md; plugins/dark-factory/templates/base/docs/review-contract.md; plugins/dark-factory/workflows/README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Independence is contextual and role-based, not separate infrastructure or empirical proof of defect detection.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: adapt the delivery factory&#39;s available organizational capability as its project environment changes. - External distinction: level inspection detects deployment/production configuration and growth to multiple human contributors; module manifests distinguish client production and monitoring requirements. - Future / prospective distinction: those signals motivate future unattended operation, client support/monitoring or a changed team-control arrangement, rather than another requested product fix. - Adaptation option generated: level report proposes the next structural step and its consequences; shipped add/provision paths identify concrete capability modules and their prerequisites. - Path back into current capability / S3: owner invokes the selected add/provision skill; file installation, seam adaptation, tests and status checks make the new module available to subsequent project operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a factory outgrowing its supported operating arrangement as production/client/team demands change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select whether and how the proposed structural capability is adopted. - Decision owner: project owner; the level/add skills explicitly reserve the selection and structural step to that owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: level.py signals/report/hint, module manifest, installer, project checks and fleet provisioning. - Closure path: environmental evidence → report/options to owner → explicit chosen structural command → shipped module installation/configuration/test/status → changed current factory capability. - Boundary reachability: level SessionStart hint and explicitly invoked level/add commands ship with the plugin; installer copies real modules, not future roadmap placeholders. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent chooses adaptation; neither deterministic hints nor a model implementing the chosen installation owns the adoption decision. - Evidence: plugins/dark-factory/scripts/level.py; plugins/dark-factory/skills/level/SKILL.md; plugins/dark-factory/skills/add/SKILL.md; plugins/dark-factory/scripts/add-module.sh; plugins/dark-factory/hooks/hooks.json. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium, bounded to the documented operator-led installation/return path. - Caveats: No autonomous S4 or empirical adaptation outcome is claimed. Vulnerability repair of a customer product and ordinary feature planning remain S1, not the credited S4 witness.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: close changes to the delivery factory&#39;s ultimate delegation/review policy. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether the project permits solo/standard/team delivery authority, including mandatory human approval and whether agents may weaken governing fences. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the local project owner using the explicitly human-invoked level command; no agent-owned alternative is supplied. - Return-to-operation path: UserPromptSubmit records an exact project/target-bound expiring one-use grant; level.py applies the authorized fence change; guard reads the stricter working/main fence so weakening binds only after integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tension between delivery freedom/unattended work and the project&#39;s reserved governance constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: authorize a change to the standing governance fence, rather than approve an individual operational command. - Decision owner: legitimate project owner; unattended/model-launched attempts cannot create the first-party prompt grant. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: level skill, prompt hook, grant store/TTL, atomic fence update, protected merge path and PreToolUse guard. - Closure path: policy-change proposal → owner explicitly names target and confirms → exact grant consumed → effective fence changed → later delivery/merge actions governed by returned policy. - Boundary reachability: level and guard are installed plugin hooks; the explicit level skill is disabled for model invocation and is the documented policy-change route. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human authority decides the standing policy. A hook denial, ordinary release acceptance or permission dialog alone is not this S5 witness. - Evidence: plugins/dark-factory/skills/level/SKILL.md; plugins/dark-factory/scripts/level.py; plugins/dark-factory/scripts/guard.py; plugins/dark-factory/hooks/hooks.json. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the implemented parent policy route. - Caveats: This is local configured-project ultimate policy, not authority over the host/provider or the OSS organization. Hooks are explicitly not a complete security sandbox.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/66623e14b849320d16f382ccf97a21476119d081/assessments/da/dark-factory.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 66623e14b849320d16f382ccf97a21476119d081\">66623e1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:36:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MUST-Company-AX-Booster/Open-Agent-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MUST-Company-AX-Booster/Open-Agent-Harness/tree/2b17fda4b5f0fe55dd577913aad8bd5febd4ba92\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2b17fda4b5f0fe55dd577913aad8bd5febd4ba92\">2b17fda &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Answer team/project queries, interpret evidence and optionally draft/execute authorized Jira changes through a model/tool feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous messages, changing project facts, incomplete histories, source inconsistencies, connector responses and failed searches. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose relevant sources and search queries, reconcile newer versus older facts, decide whether the requested response or a next lookup is needed. - Decision owner: Model-bearing Hermes Agent session, using first-party team-history skill and linked tool responses. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pinned Hermes tool loop, teamdb/Jira connectors, collector, project instructions, permission and write gates. - Closure path: User question → Hermes model selects a source/tool → reads raw history and task evidence → revises query/reasoning → returns cited answer or asks permission for an action. - Boundary reachability: Compose actually starts hermes gateway run with configured model and mounts SOUL.md plus team-history skill; pinned Hermes is a checked-in gitlink built by the standard distribution, not a hypothetical provider API. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent selects which evidence matters and the response/action; a database, polling loop and optional model selector cannot make those semantic choices. - Evidence: Compose, team-history decisions/tool search, pinned Hermes agent entry. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the standard model/tool composition; no external Slack/API-key live replay was run. - Caveats: A collector or teamdb server alone cannot own the task operation. S1 depends on a user-configured model provider and source access.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately owned regulation of mutual interference among multiple independent operational cells is established in the shipped Open Agent deployment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential competing subagent actions and shared sources are limited by tool scopes, provider caps and simple request order rather than an active peer-correction loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Detect conflicting commitments of separately acting units and feed a corrective coordination choice to them; not established. - Decision owner: Hermes parent may launch independent research children, but they return results directly without a shipped inter-child conflict adjudication relation; fixed tool/session caps are deterministic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegation.maxconcurrentchildren, subagent iteration caps, platform toolsets, read-only shared history and allowed SQL writes. - Closure path: Child results return to the lead and are synthesized as a single answer (S3); no separate inter-child collision detection → coordination decision → revised commitments is traced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead receiving parallel facts is not evidence that agents regulate interference among their work, and six mascot roles are expressly decorative. - Evidence: README mascot and architecture boundary, delegation caps/toolsets, router&#39;s bounded research parallelism. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high for the configured Open Agent distribution. - Caveats: Upstream Hermes may support additional collaboration modes, but they are not credited unless installed and reachable in this project&#39;s actual distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: For one complex user request, the lead Hermes model sees the full requested outcome, permitted sources, Todo plan, available helper results and current clarification/Jira approval status. - Current-control decision scope: Split multi-source research into independent agent tasks, prioritize searches, choose whether further evidence is needed, and integrate subordinate findings into one owner-facing project answer. - Function: Organize current cross-source and delegated research commitments for a whole bounded user task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple overlapping sources, limited child budget, insufficient evidence, conflicting status claims and slow tool results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose 3–6 Todo steps, which independent research tasks merit parallel delegation, wait for helper returns, and change next evidence requests or answer based on combined findings. - Decision owner: Lead Hermes model (not Jev classifier); user retains permission to approve externally visible Jira mutations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: oarouter complex-task guidance, pinned Hermes Todo/delegation tools, read-only search, child caps, Jira write approval and project charter. - Closure path: Whole question and available sources → model chooses subtask/resource allocation → parallel agents and tools return evidence → lead decides what still needs verification and whether to deliver, update plan or request consent. - Boundary reachability: delegation is explicitly in the Slack platform toolset, and the model-facing complex-task PLAN is supplied in the documented configured Jev mode; ordinary Hermes delegation remains available independently of that optional selector. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses work partition and integration; the router merely classifies message complexity/model tier, and the three-child cap only limits concurrency. - Evidence: model-facing PLAN and routing hook, enabled delegation and limits, pinned Hermes delegation lifecycle. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; functionality is source-visible, but a full multi-agent Slack session was not replayed. - Caveats: A single trivial lookup has no active S3 team-control demand. An unconfigured Jev ladder does not supply a model-routing decision, but does not remove Hermes&#39;s normal agent/delegation path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No shipped independently owned complementary audit of the lead/worker&#39;s task claims with distinct evidence and corrective return is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False project status answers and unreliable AI summaries; accuracy guidance requests multiple sources but does not create a separate audit organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently challenge a producer&#39;s claims using raw primary evidence and return corrective findings to the controller; no distinct role or governed path installed. - Decision owner: The same Hermes assistant gathers, cross-checks and answers facts; user confirmations and SQL constraints do not decide an independent audit verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Source citations, team-history chronological checks, Jira status lookups and nightly summaries. - Closure path: Same agent re-searches evidence before replying; no audit actor independent of producer or guaranteed corrective channel from a separate verifier. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reading multiple sources is normal S1 answer verification, and delegated research facts are not automatically an independently audited production claim. - Evidence: SOUL accuracy rules, history search procedure, config toolsets. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high for the shipped deployment. - Caveats: An independently configured reviewer could be added to Hermes outside this deployment; that is not established by default.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective environmental scanning that autonomously changes the assistant&#39;s future operating repertoire is demonstrated in the actual configured modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New team discussions and changing project facts are retained as summaries but not converted into future capability/strategy updates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a revised future operational strategy or skill configuration from external change and return it into future task control; not established. - Decision owner: Nightly Hermes model creates a retrospective channel summary, while a human configures integrations and project identity; neither is shown deciding and applying future operating adaptation in this distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: summaries table, team-history search, Cron trigger and model-selected tools for current queries. - Closure path: Yesterday&#39;s messages → model summary → next query may retrieve facts; no generated adaptation option changes the future monitoring or agent capability policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Storage/search that improves future factual recall is not itself an S4 outside-and-then adaptation decision. Optional Jev per-message model routing optimizes current question processing, not strategic capability learning. - Evidence: nightly summaries prompt, team-history retrieval, router runtime. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high within pinned deployed modes. - Caveats: Teams may manually edit future tasks after reading summaries; that would require separately established adaptation ownership/return to change this state.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What the self-hosted Open Agent is for, whose project it serves, which product/team terms it adopts and which standing source/action policies bind its replies. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Legitimate installation/project owner edits the mounted project SOUL.md and chooses integration/authorization configuration during setup; the model cannot authoritatively rewrite that read-only-mounted identity file through its disabled file/terminal tools. - Return-to-operation path: Owner changes project charter in hermes/SOUL.md → Compose mounts it into Hermes&#39;s home as read-only → pinned Hermes loadsoulmd inserts its content into subsequent system context → future agent replies and source choices observe the owner&#39;s identity/policy. - Function: Maintain the assistant&#39;s local project-team purpose and binding editorial/source boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes to team identity, authoritative project sources and enduring desired response/governance rules across tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Define/replace the durable team-agent purpose and source policy, independent of one current lookup. - Decision owner: Installation owner / authorized project maintainer, not agent subtask router or external model provider. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Setup skill, editable SOUL.md, read-only volume mount, Hermes first-party identity loader, platform toolsets. - Closure path: Parent decides a policy/identity change → edits persistent charter and restarts/reloads as needed → Hermes assembles revised model prompt → subsequent operational decisions follow it. - Boundary reachability: The standard Compose mounts this exact SOUL file read-only and the pinned source at gitlink f97608f loads it as primary identity; no optional outside reviewer or local test-only code is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The human changes binding project identity; the model uses it but cannot give itself that ultimate authority through ordinary Slack tool calls. - Evidence: project SOUL and About-project block, setup instructions, read-only mount and tool denial, pinned Hermes SOUL loader. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for local parent-authorized charter return, with no provider-backed behavioral measurement. - Caveats: S5 parent mode is the durable project identity, not generic API-key setup, ticket approval or maintainer GitHub governance. Installing a third-party skill cannot authorize contrary identity changes.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6d02e14f075b317d16659690f3afc091eb433623/assessments/mu/must-company-ax-booster-open-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6d02e14f075b317d16659690f3afc091eb433623\">6d02e14 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:10:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xingjian-bai/opal\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OPAL</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xingjian-bai/opal/tree/e999c21ee1a05891ea09d7d29bf2cd6597ca686b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e999c21ee1a05891ea09d7d29bf2cd6597ca686b\">e999c21 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out the training-optimizer task through model-chosen executable updater/analysis behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Loss/gradient dynamics, parameter roles, code failures and the operator&#39;s fixed training goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Keep or edit updater/analysis programs and choose the corresponding next optimization behavior. - Decision owner: The model-backed AgentWorker decision actor in supported live backends. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persistent backend session, decision workspace, syntax/closure checks, program loader, update-boundary installation, DDP transport, trial snapshots and rollback. - Closure path: Agent edits are collected from the workspace, checked, installed as active programs and executed on subsequent updates; actual outcomes/errors and adoption status are exported into later decisions. - Boundary reachability: wrap instantiates the first-party worker/backend and supplies its prompt/workspace; apply and install make completed choices active. The repository owns the observation-to-decision-to-training organization even though a coding CLI supplies the actor runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves the unchanged starting optimizer or keep backend and deterministic safeguards; the discretionary program choices do not occur. - Evidence: opal/wrapper.py; opal/agent.py; opal/backends/init.py; opal/backends/claude.py; opal/programs.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection establishes the shipped path, not successful live provider execution or product effectiveness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separate S2 inter-operational-unit stabilization organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential rank disagreement and stale asynchronous program answers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right and closed feedback path established. - Decision owner: No first-party function-specific decisive owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DDP synchronization, agree/checkranks, one worker in flight and file-level base merge. - Closure path: The inspected paths do not return a decision closing this metasystem function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ranks/parameter groups are replicas or parts of one logical training operation. Version agreement and stale-answer merge preserve one optimizer&#39;s consistency; they do not establish a regulator of interference among distinct autonomous S1 units. - Evidence: opal/wrapper.py; opal/agent.py; opal/misc/ddp.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection establishes the shipped path, not successful live provider execution or product effectiveness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately established whole-system current-control function at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Decision spending, waiting time, current training constraints and task failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right and closed feedback path established. - Decision owner: The operator selects fixed constraints; the agent owns bounded optimizer decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: budgetleft/costreached, schedule/waiting policy, watchdog, program adoption and recovery. - Closure path: The inspected paths do not return a decision closing this metasystem function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses local optimizer behavior; the wrapper enforces host-selected budget, horizon and safety rules. No whole-system organizational resource/commitment bargaining or intervention mandate is supplied. Parameter-wise learning rates remain task algorithm choices, not S3 merely because they allocate step sizes. - Evidence: opal/wrapper.py; opal/schedule.py; opal/misc/waiting.py; opal/backends/base.py; opal/prompts/neutral.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection establishes the shipped path, not successful live provider execution or product effectiveness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Provide complementary evidence challenging whether evaluation measurements reflect lasting training progress rather than temporary parameter substitution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An updater can move weights for a validation measurement and restore them afterward, misleading ordinary reported performance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Detect/report suspicious parameter round trips and enable an independently mandated reviewer/controller to judge their significance and corrective action. - Decision owner: The shipped Integrity sampler/analyzer deterministically owns diagnostic flags; an autonomous investigating audit actor and corrective mandate still require composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Seeded tensor samples/ring buffers, validation-opened windows, versioned evalintegrity events, stderr warnings and HTML report banner. - Closure path: Flags reach operator-facing events/report and direct the operator to inspect programs before trusting metrics. Automatic intervention and an autonomous review-to-control return are not shipped; this is the remaining constructor obligation. - Boundary reachability: With validation=N, evalintegrity defaults on in the real wrapper. reportvalidation opens a window and per-update recording resolves it; report.integrityflag consumes the resulting events. This is runtime code rather than only a test fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The source supplies an audit-specific independent reality-access and diagnostic/report path, rather than a generic log hook. It does not supply agent-owned audit judgment or correction, so C rather than A is credited. - Evidence: opal/misc/integrity.py; opal/wrapper.py; opal/report.py; tests/testevalintegrity.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded constructor claim. - Caveats: The agent is not told of the check, but generated Python is not proven unable to evade it. Flags do not stop/revert training; warnings alone are not completed autonomous audit closure. - Claim being audited: That the observed validation performance describes the durably trained model produced by the active updater. - Ordinary reporting path: Generated analysis reports and scalar validation measurements delivered to the optimization worker. - Complementary access path: Wrapper-owned sampling of actual parameter entries before/after externally reported validation, independent of agent-written analysis.py. - Independence boundary: The sampler and flag/report code are outside the generated programs and hidden from the agent prompt; testtheagentisnottold corroborates the documented separation. This is bounded access separation, not a security proof. - Who acts on findings: The operator is prompted to inspect saved programs. An application must compose an independent audit actor/authority and returned corrective control to close autonomous enactment.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Develop and adopt prospective optimizer/observation capability changes from the training environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing loss/gradient behavior, validation-versus-training divergence, performance cost and the remaining future update horizon. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Interpret the observed trajectory, form a predicted adaptation option, keep or rewrite the active optimizer/analysis, and revise that option after its consequences. - Decision owner: The persistent model-backed decision worker owns the bounded adaptation choice; the operator fixes the larger training identity and constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Training observations/inbox, versioned programs, syntax/build checks, apply/install, trial/fallback machinery and prior-outcome messages. - Closure path: The prompt requires predicted effect and falsification timing; AgentWorker passes current evidence and prior outcomes to the model. Completed option files become the active updater/analysis at a boundary. Their subsequent training effects/reversions return in the next request and prediction comparison. - Boundary reachability: This loop is wired by wrap/AgentWorker in the standard live backend mode; submit exports losses/reports/horizon, apply/install execute adopted files, and formatmessage/previousanswer report consequences. No user-written adaptation controller or adjacent research agent is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime can enforce a cap or reject code, but cannot select the prospective algorithmic response without the model. The same actor may perform S1 work and S4 adaptation; implementation co-location does not erase the distinct environment-to-capability role. - Evidence: opal/prompts/neutral.md; opal/agent.py; opal/wrapper.py; opal/programs.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high at the declared optimizer-run boundary. - Caveats: This is within-run adaptation of deployed optimizer capability. Merely writing code or updating memory would not suffice; the credited evidence includes explicit future prediction, executable adoption and subsequent environmental feedback. No empirical improvement or cross-task transfer is established. - External distinction: Actual training-workload loss/gradient/validation and latency distinctions arrive from outside the optimizer decision cell; the operator-fixed model/data/loss are its environment. - Future / prospective distinction: totalupdates and remaining decisions constrain the horizon; the prompt requires a predicted effect, by when, and what would falsify it, then comparison with observed outcomes. - Adaptation option generated: A kept or revised updater.py/analysis.py (and helper modules), changing the live update rule and/or the observation capability. - Path back into current capability / S3: wrapper.apply checks/merges the option and install replaces the active runtime objects; later updates use them and submit returns their consequences. This is a capability-return path, without claiming a separately agent-owned S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity/policy adjudication path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Proposals to change training purpose, data/model/loss or permitted optimizer scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive right and closed feedback path established. - Decision owner: Operator/developer own the initial training purpose and constraints; no first-party runtime S5 organization is supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static prompt/spec, backend workspace policy, code diagnostics and precision contracts. - Closure path: The inspected paths do not return a decision closing this metasystem function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator defines the goal and immutable training boundaries in advance. Ordinary notes, cost approval and code boundary rules do not provide an identity-level issue referral, legitimate authoritative resolution and returned governance loop. - Evidence: opal/prompts/neutral.md; opal/backends/claude.py; opal/misc/boundary.py; opal/wrapper.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source inspection establishes the shipped path, not successful live provider execution or product effectiveness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/52222052384204425c189f7f913fcd1cbad74bbf/assessments/op/opal.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 52222052384204425c189f7f913fcd1cbad74bbf\">5222205 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
